window.AWS_BEGINNER_COURSE = [{"title":"Introduction - AWS Certified Solutions Architect Associate","subtitle":"Know what you are learning and why the architecture mindset matters.","lessons":[{"title":"Start here: Introduction - AWS Certified Solutions Architect Associate","duration":8,"lead":"Understand the SAA-C03 objective, the architecture-first mindset, and how to use this course effectively.","plain":"In this section, focus first on the job these services perform. Understand the SAA-C03 objective, the architecture-first mindset, and how to use this course effectively. Do not memorize every option yet; learn the basic problem, the normal solution, and the vocabulary used in architecture diagrams.","analogy":"Learning AWS is like learning to design a building: first understand the purpose and constraints, then select the right parts.","flow":["How the SAA-C03 exam thinks","Build a decision framework","How to study with this lab","Common distractor patterns"],"points":["The exam rewards choosing a design that satisfies every stated requirement, not merely naming a compatible service.","Qualifiers such as most cost-effective, least operational effort, and highly available determine the best answer.","Unanswered questions are incorrect, so eliminate distractors and make an informed choice.","Build service-selection rules instead of memorizing isolated limits."],"example":"A scenario may allow several technically valid services, but only one meets the stated availability, security, operational-effort, and cost priorities together.","terms":[["How the SAA-C03 exam thinks","Translate scenario wording into architecture requirements and rank the constraints."],["Build a decision framework","Use repeatable service-selection rules instead of memorizing product descriptions."],["How to study with this lab","Combine short lessons, interactive labs, section checks, and timed mock exams."]],"check":{"q":"A question asks for the most operationally efficient solution. What should you prioritize?","options":["The service with the most configuration options","A managed service that satisfies every requirement","The newest AWS service","A design using the most AWS services"],"correct":1,"explain":"Operational efficiency usually favors managed services and fewer components, provided all requirements are met."}},{"title":"Architect view: Introduction - AWS Certified Solutions Architect Associate","duration":9,"lead":"Turn the basic service knowledge into a repeatable architecture decision.","plain":"A solutions architect starts with the requirement rather than a favorite service. Read the requirements in this order: business constraint → availability and durability → performance → security → operational effort → cost. Be careful of this common mistake: Selecting the most feature-rich architecture when a simpler managed option meets the stated requirements.","analogy":"A good architect is like a doctor: understand the symptoms and constraints before choosing the treatment.","flow":["Read the requirement","Remove unsuitable choices","Compare tradeoffs","Explain the final choice"],"points":["How the SAA-C03 exam thinks: Translate scenario wording into architecture requirements and rank the constraints.","Build a decision framework: Use repeatable service-selection rules instead of memorizing product descriptions.","How to study with this lab: Combine short lessons, interactive labs, section checks, and timed mock exams.","Common distractor patterns: Recognize answers that are technically possible but violate cost, availability, or operational requirements.","Decision rule: Read the requirements in this order: business constraint → availability and durability → performance → security → operational effort → cost."],"example":"A scenario may allow several technically valid services, but only one meets the stated availability, security, operational-effort, and cost priorities together.","terms":[["Common distractor patterns","Recognize answers that are technically possible but violate cost, availability, or operational requirements."],["Tradeoff","A benefit gained while accepting a cost or limitation."],["Tradeoff","A benefit gained while accepting a cost or limitation."]],"check":{"q":"What should guide the architecture choice in this section?","options":["The stated requirements and tradeoffs","The newest AWS service","The option with the longest name","Using every available feature"],"correct":0,"explain":"Service choices should be justified by requirements, constraints, and tradeoffs—not novelty or feature count."}}]},{"title":"Course materials and study method","subtitle":"Use the course, notes, checks, and labs effectively.","lessons":[{"title":"Start here: Course materials and study method","duration":8,"lead":"Learn how to use this website as a guided course instead of treating it as a list of services.","plain":"In this section, focus first on the job these services perform. Learn how to use this website as a guided course instead of treating it as a list of services. Do not memorize every option yet; learn the basic problem, the normal solution, and the vocabulary used in architecture diagrams.","analogy":"A good study plan is like a gym routine: short repeated sessions work better than one exhausting day.","flow":["Use the course in order","How to take notes","When to use the labs","When to start exam practice"],"points":["Study the sections in order the first time.","Finish one short lesson before opening its related lab.","Write down why a service fits a requirement instead of memorizing only its name.","Use the Exam workspace only after the Study workspace is reasonably familiar."],"example":"After reading an IAM lesson, explain the difference between a user and a role, answer the check, then open the IAM simulator.","terms":[["Use the course in order","Build knowledge gradually so later architecture sections have a foundation."],["How to take notes","Record service job, best use case, limits, and common confusion."],["When to use the labs","Open a simulator after the concept is clear enough to predict what should happen."]],"check":{"q":"What is the best first-pass study routine?","options":["Read a lesson, explain it, answer the check, then try the optional lab","Memorize all service names before reading","Take the same mock exam repeatedly","Skip every unfamiliar term"],"correct":0,"explain":"A small repeatable learning cycle builds understanding before exam practice."}},{"title":"Architect view: Course materials and study method","duration":9,"lead":"Turn the basic service knowledge into a repeatable architecture decision.","plain":"A solutions architect starts with the requirement rather than a favorite service. Read, explain the idea in your own words, answer the check, then use the optional lab to make the concept concrete. Be careful of this common mistake: Jumping directly to mock exams before understanding the basic service jobs.","analogy":"A good architect is like a doctor: understand the symptoms and constraints before choosing the treatment.","flow":["Read the requirement","Remove unsuitable choices","Compare tradeoffs","Explain the final choice"],"points":["Use the course in order: Build knowledge gradually so later architecture sections have a foundation.","How to take notes: Record service job, best use case, limits, and common confusion.","When to use the labs: Open a simulator after the concept is clear enough to predict what should happen.","When to start exam practice: Move to exam questions only after completing the main service sections.","Decision rule: Read, explain the idea in your own words, answer the check, then use the optional lab to make the concept concrete."],"example":"After reading an IAM lesson, explain the difference between a user and a role, answer the check, then open the IAM simulator.","terms":[["When to start exam practice","Move to exam questions only after completing the main service sections."],["Tradeoff","A benefit gained while accepting a cost or limitation."],["Tradeoff","A benefit gained while accepting a cost or limitation."]],"check":{"q":"What should guide the architecture choice in this section?","options":["The stated requirements and tradeoffs","The newest AWS service","The option with the longest name","Using every available feature"],"correct":0,"explain":"Service choices should be justified by requirements, constraints, and tradeoffs—not novelty or feature count."}}]},{"title":"Getting started with AWS","subtitle":"Understand the AWS account, console, Regions, Availability Zones, and service scope.","lessons":[{"title":"Start here: Getting started with AWS","duration":8,"lead":"Learn Regions, Availability Zones, edge locations, and how geography affects architecture choices.","plain":"In this section, focus first on the job these services perform. Learn Regions, Availability Zones, edge locations, and how geography affects architecture choices. Do not memorize every option yet; learn the basic problem, the normal solution, and the vocabulary used in architecture diagrams.","analogy":"Think of a Region as a city and Availability Zones as separate buildings in that city.","flow":["Regions and Availability Zones","Edge locations and regional edge caches","Selecting an AWS Region","Global versus regional services"],"points":["A Region is a separate geographic area containing multiple isolated Availability Zones.","Multi-AZ designs protect against an Availability Zone failure; multi-Region designs address broader regional requirements.","Some services are global, while many resources are regional or zonal.","Choose a Region based on compliance, latency, service availability, and cost."],"example":"Run web servers in two Availability Zones in one Region so one AZ failure does not stop the application.","terms":[["Regions and Availability Zones","Distinguish geographic Regions from isolated Availability Zones."],["Edge locations and regional edge caches","Understand where CloudFront and edge services process requests."],["Selecting an AWS Region","Balance latency, compliance, service availability, and pricing."]],"check":{"q":"A workload must survive the loss of one data center with minimal complexity. Which design is appropriate?","options":["One large EC2 instance","Instances across two Availability Zones","Two AWS accounts in one Availability Zone","Two edge locations"],"correct":1,"explain":"Availability Zones are isolated data-center groupings within a Region and are the normal unit for regional high availability."}},{"title":"Architect view: Getting started with AWS","duration":9,"lead":"Turn the basic service knowledge into a repeatable architecture decision.","plain":"A solutions architect starts with the requirement rather than a favorite service. Use multiple Availability Zones for normal high availability; use multiple Regions only when the recovery, sovereignty, or latency requirement justifies the extra complexity. Be careful of this common mistake: Assuming that deploying two instances in the same Availability Zone creates a highly available architecture.","analogy":"A good architect is like a doctor: understand the symptoms and constraints before choosing the treatment.","flow":["Read the requirement","Remove unsuitable choices","Compare tradeoffs","Explain the final choice"],"points":["Regions and Availability Zones: Distinguish geographic Regions from isolated Availability Zones.","Edge locations and regional edge caches: Understand where CloudFront and edge services process requests.","Selecting an AWS Region: Balance latency, compliance, service availability, and pricing.","Global versus regional services: Identify resource scope and avoid incorrect failover assumptions.","Decision rule: Use multiple Availability Zones for normal high availability; use multiple Regions only when the recovery, sovereignty, or latency requirement justifies the extra complexity."],"example":"Run web servers in two Availability Zones in one Region so one AZ failure does not stop the application.","terms":[["Global versus regional services","Identify resource scope and avoid incorrect failover assumptions."],["Tradeoff","A benefit gained while accepting a cost or limitation."],["Tradeoff","A benefit gained while accepting a cost or limitation."]],"check":{"q":"What should guide the architecture choice in this section?","options":["The stated requirements and tradeoffs","The newest AWS service","The option with the longest name","Using every available feature"],"correct":0,"explain":"Service choices should be justified by requirements, constraints, and tradeoffs—not novelty or feature count."}}]},{"title":"IAM & AWS CLI","subtitle":"Control access and learn the basic ways humans and programs interact with AWS.","lessons":[{"title":"Start here: IAM & AWS CLI","duration":8,"lead":"Build secure access models with identities, roles, policies, federation, Organizations, and temporary credentials.","plain":"In this section, focus first on the job these services perform. Build secure access models with identities, roles, policies, federation, Organizations, and temporary credentials. Do not memorize every option yet; learn the basic problem, the normal solution, and the vocabulary used in architecture diagrams.","analogy":"IAM is like an office badge system that controls which rooms and actions each person or machine may use.","flow":["Users, groups, roles, and policies","IAM policy evaluation","MFA, password policies, and access keys","AWS CLI and credential providers"],"points":["IAM evaluates identity policies, resource policies, permission boundaries, SCPs, and explicit denies together.","Roles provide temporary credentials and are preferred for workloads and cross-account access.","Service control policies set maximum permissions for accounts in AWS Organizations; they do not grant permissions.","Protect the root user with MFA and avoid routine root access."],"example":"An EC2 application receives temporary credentials from a role instead of storing an IAM user's long-lived access key.","terms":[["Users, groups, roles, and policies","Choose the right identity type and understand policy attachment."],["IAM policy evaluation","Apply explicit deny, least privilege, and policy intersections."],["MFA, password policies, and access keys","Protect human identities and remove unnecessary long-lived credentials."]],"check":{"q":"An EC2 application needs access to one S3 bucket without stored access keys. What should you use?","options":["An IAM user access key in user data","An instance profile with an IAM role","The root user credentials","A bucket made public"],"correct":1,"explain":"An EC2 instance profile supplies temporary role credentials to the application without embedding long-lived secrets."}},{"title":"Architect view: IAM & AWS CLI","duration":9,"lead":"Turn the basic service knowledge into a repeatable architecture decision.","plain":"A solutions architect starts with the requirement rather than a favorite service. Use users only for exceptional long-lived human identities; prefer federation for people and roles for workloads or cross-account access. Be careful of this common mistake: Treating an SCP as a policy that directly grants access to a principal.","analogy":"A good architect is like a doctor: understand the symptoms and constraints before choosing the treatment.","flow":["Read the requirement","Remove unsuitable choices","Compare tradeoffs","Explain the final choice"],"points":["Users, groups, roles, and policies: Choose the right identity type and understand policy attachment.","IAM policy evaluation: Apply explicit deny, least privilege, and policy intersections.","MFA, password policies, and access keys: Protect human identities and remove unnecessary long-lived credentials.","AWS CLI and credential providers: Understand profiles, environment variables, and role assumption.","Decision rule: Use users only for exceptional long-lived human identities; prefer federation for people and roles for workloads or cross-account access."],"example":"An EC2 application receives temporary credentials from a role instead of storing an IAM user's long-lived access key.","terms":[["AWS CLI and credential providers","Understand profiles, environment variables, and role assumption."],["Cross-account access and STS","Use trust policies and temporary credentials for account boundaries."],["Organizations, SCPs, and IAM Identity Center","Centralize workforce access and establish permission guardrails."]],"check":{"q":"What should guide the architecture choice in this section?","options":["The stated requirements and tradeoffs","The newest AWS service","The option with the longest name","Using every available feature"],"correct":0,"explain":"Service choices should be justified by requirements, constraints, and tradeoffs—not novelty or feature count."},"lab":"iam.html"}]},{"title":"EC2 Fundamentals","subtitle":"Learn what a virtual server is and how to launch, connect, secure, and pay for it.","lessons":[{"title":"Start here: EC2 Fundamentals","duration":8,"lead":"Understand instance families, purchasing options, networking, metadata, bootstrap, and core EC2 behavior.","plain":"In this section, focus first on the job these services perform. Understand instance families, purchasing options, networking, metadata, bootstrap, and core EC2 behavior. Do not memorize every option yet; learn the basic problem, the normal solution, and the vocabulary used in architecture diagrams.","analogy":"EC2 is like renting a computer in an AWS data center and choosing its size, operating system, network, and price model.","flow":["Instance types and families","AMI, user data, and instance metadata","Security groups and ENIs","Public, private, and Elastic IP addresses"],"points":["Instance families are optimized for general purpose, compute, memory, storage, accelerated computing, or other workload profiles.","User data normally runs during the first boot and is commonly used for bootstrap automation.","Security groups are stateful virtual firewalls associated with elastic network interfaces.","Spot Instances can be interrupted and suit fault-tolerant workloads."],"example":"Launch a small Linux EC2 instance, allow HTTP through its security group, and bootstrap a web page with user data.","terms":[["Instance types and families","Match CPU, memory, storage, and accelerator needs to instance families."],["AMI, user data, and instance metadata","Launch consistent servers and bootstrap them safely."],["Security groups and ENIs","Control stateful network access and understand virtual interfaces."]],"check":{"q":"A stateless batch job can tolerate interruptions and needs the lowest compute cost. Which option fits best?","options":["Dedicated Hosts","Spot Instances","On-Demand Capacity Reservations","Convertible Reserved Instances"],"correct":1,"explain":"Spot Instances provide discounted spare capacity for interruptible, fault-tolerant workloads."}},{"title":"Architect view: EC2 Fundamentals","duration":9,"lead":"Turn the basic service knowledge into a repeatable architecture decision.","plain":"A solutions architect starts with the requirement rather than a favorite service. Match the workload profile first, then choose a purchase model based on commitment, interruption tolerance, and flexibility. Be careful of this common mistake: Choosing Spot Instances for a non-interruptible single-instance database because the price is lower.","analogy":"A good architect is like a doctor: understand the symptoms and constraints before choosing the treatment.","flow":["Read the requirement","Remove unsuitable choices","Compare tradeoffs","Explain the final choice"],"points":["Instance types and families: Match CPU, memory, storage, and accelerator needs to instance families.","AMI, user data, and instance metadata: Launch consistent servers and bootstrap them safely.","Security groups and ENIs: Control stateful network access and understand virtual interfaces.","Public, private, and Elastic IP addresses: Know how EC2 instances receive and retain addressing.","Decision rule: Match the workload profile first, then choose a purchase model based on commitment, interruption tolerance, and flexibility."],"example":"Launch a small Linux EC2 instance, allow HTTP through its security group, and bootstrap a web page with user data.","terms":[["Public, private, and Elastic IP addresses","Know how EC2 instances receive and retain addressing."],["EC2 purchasing models","Compare On-Demand, Spot, Reserved Instances, Savings Plans, and Dedicated options."],["Tradeoff","A benefit gained while accepting a cost or limitation."]],"check":{"q":"What should guide the architecture choice in this section?","options":["The stated requirements and tradeoffs","The newest AWS service","The option with the longest name","Using every available feature"],"correct":0,"explain":"Service choices should be justified by requirements, constraints, and tradeoffs—not novelty or feature count."}}]},{"title":"EC2 - Solutions Architect Associate Level","subtitle":"Move from one server to architecture choices such as placement, lifecycle, and fleet operations.","lessons":[{"title":"Start here: EC2 - Solutions Architect Associate Level","duration":8,"lead":"Apply placement groups, hibernation, capacity controls, and automation patterns to EC2 designs.","plain":"In this section, focus first on the job these services perform. Apply placement groups, hibernation, capacity controls, and automation patterns to EC2 designs. Do not memorize every option yet; learn the basic problem, the normal solution, and the vocabulary used in architecture diagrams.","analogy":"Managing many EC2 instances is like managing a vehicle fleet: placement, lifecycle, access, and automation matter.","flow":["Placement groups","Hibernation and lifecycle behavior","Capacity Reservations and Dedicated Hosts","Systems Manager for EC2"],"points":["Cluster placement groups reduce network latency but place instances close together.","Spread placement groups separate a small number of critical instances across distinct hardware.","Partition placement groups divide large distributed systems into logical hardware partitions.","Systems Manager can manage instances without opening inbound SSH when configured correctly."],"example":"Use Systems Manager Session Manager to operate an instance without opening inbound SSH to the internet.","terms":[["Placement groups","Choose cluster, spread, or partition placement."],["Hibernation and lifecycle behavior","Understand stop, terminate, reboot, and hibernation effects."],["Capacity Reservations and Dedicated Hosts","Separate capacity assurance from billing discounts and tenancy."]],"check":{"q":"A distributed database needs groups of instances isolated from correlated hardware failure. Which placement strategy fits?","options":["Cluster placement group","Partition placement group","Dedicated Host only","Capacity Reservation"],"correct":1,"explain":"Partition placement groups expose logical partitions so distributed systems can place replicas across separated hardware groups."}},{"title":"Architect view: EC2 - Solutions Architect Associate Level","duration":9,"lead":"Turn the basic service knowledge into a repeatable architecture decision.","plain":"A solutions architect starts with the requirement rather than a favorite service. Select placement based on whether the priority is low latency, hardware isolation for a few instances, or partition-aware distributed systems. Be careful of this common mistake: Using a cluster placement group when fault isolation is the main requirement.","analogy":"A good architect is like a doctor: understand the symptoms and constraints before choosing the treatment.","flow":["Read the requirement","Remove unsuitable choices","Compare tradeoffs","Explain the final choice"],"points":["Placement groups: Choose cluster, spread, or partition placement.","Hibernation and lifecycle behavior: Understand stop, terminate, reboot, and hibernation effects.","Capacity Reservations and Dedicated Hosts: Separate capacity assurance from billing discounts and tenancy.","Systems Manager for EC2: Operate instances using Session Manager, Run Command, inventory, and patching.","Decision rule: Select placement based on whether the priority is low latency, hardware isolation for a few instances, or partition-aware distributed systems."],"example":"Use Systems Manager Session Manager to operate an instance without opening inbound SSH to the internet.","terms":[["Systems Manager for EC2","Operate instances using Session Manager, Run Command, inventory, and patching."],["Tradeoff","A benefit gained while accepting a cost or limitation."],["Tradeoff","A benefit gained while accepting a cost or limitation."]],"check":{"q":"What should guide the architecture choice in this section?","options":["The stated requirements and tradeoffs","The newest AWS service","The option with the longest name","Using every available feature"],"correct":0,"explain":"Service choices should be justified by requirements, constraints, and tradeoffs—not novelty or feature count."}}]},{"title":"EC2 Instance Storage","subtitle":"Choose persistent disks, temporary disks, and shared file systems.","lessons":[{"title":"Start here: EC2 Instance Storage","duration":8,"lead":"Choose block, ephemeral, and shared file storage for compute workloads.","plain":"In this section, focus first on the job these services perform. Choose block, ephemeral, and shared file storage for compute workloads. Do not memorize every option yet; learn the basic problem, the normal solution, and the vocabulary used in architecture diagrams.","analogy":"Storage choices are like a laptop disk, a temporary workbench, or a shared company drive—each solves a different problem.","flow":["EBS volume types and performance","EBS snapshots and encryption","Instance store","Amazon EFS"],"points":["EBS provides persistent block storage and volumes are created in a specific Availability Zone.","Instance store is ephemeral storage physically attached to the host and is lost when the relevant instance lifecycle ends.","EFS is a managed elastic NFS file system designed for concurrent access from Linux clients.","FSx provides managed file systems optimized for Windows, Lustre, NetApp ONTAP, and OpenZFS use cases."],"example":"Use EBS for one server's persistent boot disk and EFS when many Linux servers need the same shared files.","terms":[["EBS volume types and performance","Compare general-purpose SSD, provisioned IOPS, throughput HDD, and cold HDD."],["EBS snapshots and encryption","Protect volumes, copy snapshots, and understand encryption inheritance."],["Instance store","Use ephemeral local disks for caches, buffers, and scratch data."]],"check":{"q":"Hundreds of Linux EC2 instances need concurrent access to the same POSIX file system. Which service is the normal choice?","options":["Amazon EBS","Amazon EFS","Instance store","Amazon S3 Glacier"],"correct":1,"explain":"EFS is a managed NFS service built for shared file access from multiple Linux clients."}},{"title":"Architect view: EC2 Instance Storage","duration":9,"lead":"Turn the basic service knowledge into a repeatable architecture decision.","plain":"A solutions architect starts with the requirement rather than a favorite service. Choose EBS for one-instance block storage, EFS for shared Linux file access, FSx for specialized managed file systems, and instance store for disposable high-performance scratch data. Be careful of this common mistake: Using instance store for data that must survive instance replacement.","analogy":"A good architect is like a doctor: understand the symptoms and constraints before choosing the treatment.","flow":["Read the requirement","Remove unsuitable choices","Compare tradeoffs","Explain the final choice"],"points":["EBS volume types and performance: Compare general-purpose SSD, provisioned IOPS, throughput HDD, and cold HDD.","EBS snapshots and encryption: Protect volumes, copy snapshots, and understand encryption inheritance.","Instance store: Use ephemeral local disks for caches, buffers, and scratch data.","Amazon EFS: Design shared Linux file storage across Availability Zones.","Decision rule: Choose EBS for one-instance block storage, EFS for shared Linux file access, FSx for specialized managed file systems, and instance store for disposable high-performance scratch data."],"example":"Use EBS for one server's persistent boot disk and EFS when many Linux servers need the same shared files.","terms":[["Amazon EFS","Design shared Linux file storage across Availability Zones."],["Amazon FSx families","Select Windows, Lustre, ONTAP, or OpenZFS based on protocol and workload."],["Tradeoff","A benefit gained while accepting a cost or limitation."]],"check":{"q":"What should guide the architecture choice in this section?","options":["The stated requirements and tradeoffs","The newest AWS service","The option with the longest name","Using every available feature"],"correct":0,"explain":"Service choices should be justified by requirements, constraints, and tradeoffs—not novelty or feature count."},"lab":"storage-selector.html"}]},{"title":"High Availability and Scalability: ELB & ASG","subtitle":"Keep an application available and adjust server capacity with demand.","lessons":[{"title":"Start here: High Availability and Scalability: ELB & ASG","duration":8,"lead":"Design scalable web tiers using load balancers, target groups, health checks, and scaling policies.","plain":"In this section, focus first on the job these services perform. Design scalable web tiers using load balancers, target groups, health checks, and scaling policies. Do not memorize every option yet; learn the basic problem, the normal solution, and the vocabulary used in architecture diagrams.","analogy":"A load balancer is a restaurant host distributing customers; Auto Scaling adds or removes tables and staff as demand changes.","flow":["ALB, NLB, and GWLB","Target groups and health checks","Session stickiness and cross-zone behavior","Auto Scaling groups"],"points":["Application Load Balancer operates at Layer 7 and supports host- and path-based routing.","Network Load Balancer operates at Layer 4 and is designed for very high performance and static IP requirements.","Gateway Load Balancer helps deploy and scale third-party virtual network appliances.","Auto Scaling target tracking adjusts capacity to keep a metric near a target value."],"example":"An ALB sends requests only to healthy EC2 instances while an Auto Scaling group adds capacity during a traffic spike.","terms":[["ALB, NLB, and GWLB","Choose the load balancer type from protocol and routing requirements."],["Target groups and health checks","Route only to healthy targets and coordinate health evaluation."],["Session stickiness and cross-zone behavior","Understand state, distribution, and architecture tradeoffs."]],"check":{"q":"A service needs path-based HTTP routing to multiple target groups. Which load balancer should you choose?","options":["Classic Load Balancer","Application Load Balancer","Network Load Balancer","Gateway Load Balancer"],"correct":1,"explain":"ALB provides Layer 7 host- and path-based request routing."}},{"title":"Architect view: High Availability and Scalability: ELB & ASG","duration":9,"lead":"Turn the basic service knowledge into a repeatable architecture decision.","plain":"A solutions architect starts with the requirement rather than a favorite service. Use ALB for HTTP routing features, NLB for TCP/UDP/TLS performance or static IPs, and GWLB for transparent network appliances. Be careful of this common mistake: Assuming a load balancer automatically replaces unhealthy instances without an Auto Scaling group or another capacity manager.","analogy":"A good architect is like a doctor: understand the symptoms and constraints before choosing the treatment.","flow":["Read the requirement","Remove unsuitable choices","Compare tradeoffs","Explain the final choice"],"points":["ALB, NLB, and GWLB: Choose the load balancer type from protocol and routing requirements.","Target groups and health checks: Route only to healthy targets and coordinate health evaluation.","Session stickiness and cross-zone behavior: Understand state, distribution, and architecture tradeoffs.","Auto Scaling groups: Set launch templates, minimum, desired, and maximum capacity.","Decision rule: Use ALB for HTTP routing features, NLB for TCP/UDP/TLS performance or static IPs, and GWLB for transparent network appliances."],"example":"An ALB sends requests only to healthy EC2 instances while an Auto Scaling group adds capacity during a traffic spike.","terms":[["Auto Scaling groups","Set launch templates, minimum, desired, and maximum capacity."],["Scaling policies and warmup","Compare target tracking, step scaling, scheduled scaling, and predictive scaling."],["Highly available web tier pattern","Combine multi-AZ targets, load balancing, and automated replacement."]],"check":{"q":"What should guide the architecture choice in this section?","options":["The stated requirements and tradeoffs","The newest AWS service","The option with the longest name","Using every available feature"],"correct":0,"explain":"Service choices should be justified by requirements, constraints, and tradeoffs—not novelty or feature count."},"lab":"load-balancing.html"}]},{"title":"AWS Fundamentals: RDS + Aurora + ElastiCache","subtitle":"Use managed relational databases, replicas, failover, and caches.","lessons":[{"title":"Start here: AWS Fundamentals: RDS + Aurora + ElastiCache","duration":8,"lead":"Design managed relational database architectures with availability, read scaling, backups, and caching.","plain":"In this section, focus first on the job these services perform. Design managed relational database architectures with availability, read scaling, backups, and caching. Do not memorize every option yet; learn the basic problem, the normal solution, and the vocabulary used in architecture diagrams.","analogy":"A database is the system of record; a cache is a fast nearby copy used to avoid repeating expensive work.","flow":["RDS engines and deployment choices","Multi-AZ and failover","Read replicas","Aurora architecture"],"points":["RDS Multi-AZ is primarily an availability and failover feature, not a read-scaling feature.","Read replicas scale read traffic and can support cross-Region disaster recovery patterns.","Aurora separates compute from a distributed storage layer and supports multiple replicas.","ElastiCache reduces database load for suitable cacheable data using Redis/Valkey or Memcached."],"example":"Use RDS Multi-AZ for automatic failover and a read replica when the separate requirement is read scaling.","terms":[["RDS engines and deployment choices","Select a relational engine and understand managed responsibilities."],["Multi-AZ and failover","Design for database availability without confusing standby and replica roles."],["Read replicas","Scale reads and design cross-Region replication patterns."]],"check":{"q":"An RDS workload is read-heavy but already meets its availability requirement. What is the direct scaling option?","options":["Add a read replica","Enable Multi-AZ only","Create a larger subnet","Add an internet gateway"],"correct":0,"explain":"Read replicas are designed to offload read traffic; Multi-AZ primarily provides failover."}},{"title":"Architect view: AWS Fundamentals: RDS + Aurora + ElastiCache","duration":9,"lead":"Turn the basic service knowledge into a repeatable architecture decision.","plain":"A solutions architect starts with the requirement rather than a favorite service. Use Multi-AZ for high availability, read replicas for read scaling, and ElastiCache when repeated reads or session state can be served from memory. Be careful of this common mistake: Sending application read traffic to the standby of a traditional RDS Multi-AZ deployment.","analogy":"A good architect is like a doctor: understand the symptoms and constraints before choosing the treatment.","flow":["Read the requirement","Remove unsuitable choices","Compare tradeoffs","Explain the final choice"],"points":["RDS engines and deployment choices: Select a relational engine and understand managed responsibilities.","Multi-AZ and failover: Design for database availability without confusing standby and replica roles.","Read replicas: Scale reads and design cross-Region replication patterns.","Aurora architecture: Use distributed storage, replicas, Global Database, and Serverless options.","Decision rule: Use Multi-AZ for high availability, read replicas for read scaling, and ElastiCache when repeated reads or session state can be served from memory."],"example":"Use RDS Multi-AZ for automatic failover and a read replica when the separate requirement is read scaling.","terms":[["Aurora architecture","Use distributed storage, replicas, Global Database, and Serverless options."],["Backups, snapshots, and restore","Distinguish automated backups, manual snapshots, and point-in-time recovery."],["ElastiCache patterns","Apply cache-aside, session storage, and in-memory performance designs."]],"check":{"q":"What should guide the architecture choice in this section?","options":["The stated requirements and tradeoffs","The newest AWS service","The option with the longest name","Using every available feature"],"correct":0,"explain":"Service choices should be justified by requirements, constraints, and tradeoffs—not novelty or feature count."},"lab":"database-selector.html"}]},{"title":"Route 53","subtitle":"Understand DNS and route users to healthy endpoints.","lessons":[{"title":"Start here: Route 53","duration":8,"lead":"Use DNS records, health checks, and routing policies to direct users to healthy endpoints.","plain":"In this section, focus first on the job these services perform. Use DNS records, health checks, and routing policies to direct users to healthy endpoints. Do not memorize every option yet; learn the basic problem, the normal solution, and the vocabulary used in architecture diagrams.","analogy":"DNS is the internet's address book, and Route 53 can choose which healthy destination should answer.","flow":["DNS fundamentals and record types","Hosted zones and domain registration","Route 53 routing policies","Health checks and failover"],"points":["Alias records can route the zone apex to supported AWS resources without a CNAME.","Simple routing returns one or more records without specialized health-based policy behavior.","Failover, latency, weighted, geolocation, geoproximity, and multivalue policies solve different routing requirements.","Route 53 health checks can influence DNS responses but DNS caching affects failover timing."],"example":"Use latency routing to direct global users toward a lower-latency Region, with health checks to avoid unhealthy endpoints.","terms":[["DNS fundamentals and record types","Understand A, AAAA, CNAME, alias, NS, MX, and TXT records."],["Hosted zones and domain registration","Separate DNS hosting from registrar responsibilities."],["Route 53 routing policies","Select simple, weighted, latency, failover, geolocation, geoproximity, or multivalue."]],"check":{"q":"You must send 10% of users to a new deployment and 90% to the existing one. Which policy fits?","options":["Latency routing","Weighted routing","Failover routing","Geolocation routing"],"correct":1,"explain":"Weighted routing distributes DNS responses according to configured relative weights."}},{"title":"Architect view: Route 53","duration":9,"lead":"Turn the basic service knowledge into a repeatable architecture decision.","plain":"A solutions architect starts with the requirement rather than a favorite service. Choose routing policy from the business goal: distribute percentages, minimize latency, enforce geography, fail over, or return multiple healthy values. Be careful of this common mistake: Expecting DNS failover to be instantaneous despite resolver and record TTL caching.","analogy":"A good architect is like a doctor: understand the symptoms and constraints before choosing the treatment.","flow":["Read the requirement","Remove unsuitable choices","Compare tradeoffs","Explain the final choice"],"points":["DNS fundamentals and record types: Understand A, AAAA, CNAME, alias, NS, MX, and TXT records.","Hosted zones and domain registration: Separate DNS hosting from registrar responsibilities.","Route 53 routing policies: Select simple, weighted, latency, failover, geolocation, geoproximity, or multivalue.","Health checks and failover: Build DNS failover while accounting for health evaluation and TTL.","Decision rule: Choose routing policy from the business goal: distribute percentages, minimize latency, enforce geography, fail over, or return multiple healthy values."],"example":"Use latency routing to direct global users toward a lower-latency Region, with health checks to avoid unhealthy endpoints.","terms":[["Health checks and failover","Build DNS failover while accounting for health evaluation and TTL."],["Hybrid DNS with Resolver","Connect on-premises and VPC DNS using inbound and outbound endpoints."],["Tradeoff","A benefit gained while accepting a cost or limitation."]],"check":{"q":"What should guide the architecture choice in this section?","options":["The stated requirements and tradeoffs","The newest AWS service","The option with the longest name","Using every available feature"],"correct":0,"explain":"Service choices should be justified by requirements, constraints, and tradeoffs—not novelty or feature count."},"lab":"route53-delivery.html"}]},{"title":"Classic Solutions Architecture Discussions","subtitle":"Combine core services into understandable web architectures.","lessons":[{"title":"Start here: Classic Solutions Architecture Discussions","duration":8,"lead":"Combine services into secure, resilient, high-performing, and cost-aware reference architectures.","plain":"In this section, focus first on the job these services perform. Combine services into secure, resilient, high-performing, and cost-aware reference architectures. Do not memorize every option yet; learn the basic problem, the normal solution, and the vocabulary used in architecture diagrams.","analogy":"Architecture is a blueprint showing how users, networks, compute, storage, and data services work together.","flow":["Three-tier web architecture","Event-driven architecture","Serverless web application","Data processing pipeline"],"points":["Stateless application tiers are easier to scale horizontally and replace.","Queues absorb bursts and decouple producer and consumer availability.","Caching can reduce latency and origin load but introduces freshness and invalidation tradeoffs.","Managed services usually reduce operational burden but may reduce low-level control."],"example":"A basic web app can use Route 53, CloudFront, an ALB, Auto Scaling EC2, and a managed database in private subnets.","terms":[["Three-tier web architecture","Combine Route 53, CloudFront, ALB, Auto Scaling, and managed data services."],["Event-driven architecture","Use events, queues, topics, functions, and workflow orchestration."],["Serverless web application","Design managed front ends, APIs, compute, identity, and data."]],"check":{"q":"Traffic arrives in bursts and backend workers process jobs at a steady rate. Which component best decouples the rates?","options":["Amazon SQS","A larger DNS TTL","A network ACL","An EBS snapshot"],"correct":0,"explain":"SQS buffers work so producers and consumers can operate at different rates and recover independently."}},{"title":"Architect view: Classic Solutions Architecture Discussions","duration":9,"lead":"Turn the basic service knowledge into a repeatable architecture decision.","plain":"A solutions architect starts with the requirement rather than a favorite service. Choose the simplest architecture that meets all nonfunctional requirements, then identify the failure mode of each component and remove unnecessary single points of failure. Be careful of this common mistake: Adding components because they are common in reference diagrams even when the scenario does not require them.","analogy":"A good architect is like a doctor: understand the symptoms and constraints before choosing the treatment.","flow":["Read the requirement","Remove unsuitable choices","Compare tradeoffs","Explain the final choice"],"points":["Three-tier web architecture: Combine Route 53, CloudFront, ALB, Auto Scaling, and managed data services.","Event-driven architecture: Use events, queues, topics, functions, and workflow orchestration.","Serverless web application: Design managed front ends, APIs, compute, identity, and data.","Data processing pipeline: Ingest, buffer, transform, catalog, store, and analyze data.","Decision rule: Choose the simplest architecture that meets all nonfunctional requirements, then identify the failure mode of each component and remove unnecessary single points of failure."],"example":"A basic web app can use Route 53, CloudFront, an ALB, Auto Scaling EC2, and a managed database in private subnets.","terms":[["Data processing pipeline","Ingest, buffer, transform, catalog, store, and analyze data."],["Hybrid architecture","Integrate on-premises identity, networks, DNS, storage, and migration."],["Service-selection method","Compare options against protocol, consistency, scale, resilience, operations, and cost."]],"check":{"q":"What should guide the architecture choice in this section?","options":["The stated requirements and tradeoffs","The newest AWS service","The option with the longest name","Using every available feature"],"correct":0,"explain":"Service choices should be justified by requirements, constraints, and tradeoffs—not novelty or feature count."},"lab":"autoscaling.html"}]},{"title":"Amazon S3 Introduction","subtitle":"Learn object storage, buckets, objects, and storage classes.","lessons":[{"title":"Start here: Amazon S3 Introduction","duration":8,"lead":"Master buckets, objects, durability, consistency, storage classes, and core S3 design patterns.","plain":"In this section, focus first on the job these services perform. Master buckets, objects, durability, consistency, storage classes, and core S3 design patterns. Do not memorize every option yet; learn the basic problem, the normal solution, and the vocabulary used in architecture diagrams.","analogy":"S3 is like a huge warehouse of labeled objects rather than a disk attached to one computer.","flow":["Buckets, objects, keys, and prefixes","Consistency, durability, and availability","Storage classes","Multipart upload and Transfer Acceleration"],"points":["S3 is object storage accessed through APIs rather than a normal block or POSIX file system.","Object keys identify objects within a bucket; prefixes are logical naming constructs.","S3 is designed for very high durability and provides strong read-after-write consistency.","Storage classes vary by access pattern, retrieval time, resilience scope, and minimum storage duration."],"example":"Store uploaded photos as S3 objects and keep user or order records in a database rather than treating S3 as a relational database.","terms":[["Buckets, objects, keys, and prefixes","Understand the S3 data model and namespace."],["Consistency, durability, and availability","Interpret S3 guarantees correctly in architecture questions."],["Storage classes","Choose Standard, Intelligent-Tiering, IA, One Zone-IA, and Glacier classes."]],"check":{"q":"Which workload is the strongest fit for Amazon S3?","options":["A boot volume for EC2","Shared POSIX home directories","Durable storage for millions of independent objects","Low-latency database transaction logs"],"correct":2,"explain":"S3 is designed for durable, scalable object storage."}},{"title":"Architect view: Amazon S3 Introduction","duration":9,"lead":"Turn the basic service knowledge into a repeatable architecture decision.","plain":"A solutions architect starts with the requirement rather than a favorite service. Use S3 for scalable object storage, static assets, backups, data lakes, and durable application objects—not as an EBS replacement. Be careful of this common mistake: Choosing S3 for an application that requires in-place block updates from an operating system file system.","analogy":"A good architect is like a doctor: understand the symptoms and constraints before choosing the treatment.","flow":["Read the requirement","Remove unsuitable choices","Compare tradeoffs","Explain the final choice"],"points":["Buckets, objects, keys, and prefixes: Understand the S3 data model and namespace.","Consistency, durability, and availability: Interpret S3 guarantees correctly in architecture questions.","Storage classes: Choose Standard, Intelligent-Tiering, IA, One Zone-IA, and Glacier classes.","Multipart upload and Transfer Acceleration: Improve upload efficiency for large or distant transfers.","Decision rule: Use S3 for scalable object storage, static assets, backups, data lakes, and durable application objects—not as an EBS replacement."],"example":"Store uploaded photos as S3 objects and keep user or order records in a database rather than treating S3 as a relational database.","terms":[["Multipart upload and Transfer Acceleration","Improve upload efficiency for large or distant transfers."],["Static website hosting","Know when S3 website endpoints fit and how they differ from normal REST endpoints."],["Tradeoff","A benefit gained while accepting a cost or limitation."]],"check":{"q":"What should guide the architecture choice in this section?","options":["The stated requirements and tradeoffs","The newest AWS service","The option with the longest name","Using every available feature"],"correct":0,"explain":"Service choices should be justified by requirements, constraints, and tradeoffs—not novelty or feature count."},"lab":"s3-lifecycle.html"}]},{"title":"Advanced Amazon S3","subtitle":"Use lifecycle, replication, versioning, events, and performance features.","lessons":[{"title":"Start here: Advanced Amazon S3","duration":8,"lead":"Use S3 features beyond basic object storage, including versioning, lifecycle, replication, events, and performance patterns.","plain":"In this section, focus first on the job these services perform. Use S3 features beyond basic object storage, including versioning, lifecycle, replication, events, and performance patterns. Do not memorize every option yet; learn the basic problem, the normal solution, and the vocabulary used in architecture diagrams.","analogy":"Advanced S3 is like running warehouse rules: who may enter, where copies go, and when old items move or expire.","flow":["S3 authorization model","SSE-S3, SSE-KMS, and client-side encryption","Versioning and Object Lock","Cross-Region and same-Region replication"],"points":["Versioning preserves multiple versions of an object.","Lifecycle rules transition or expire objects based on age and other filters.","Replication copies eligible objects to another bucket and requires versioning.","S3 events can invoke destinations such as SQS, SNS, Lambda, or EventBridge."],"example":"Enable versioning, encrypt objects, restrict access, replicate important data, and transition old objects to a cheaper class.","terms":[["S3 authorization model","Combine IAM, bucket policies, access points, ACL considerations, and Block Public Access."],["SSE-S3, SSE-KMS, and client-side encryption","Choose encryption and understand key-control tradeoffs."],["Versioning and Object Lock","Protect objects from accidental or malicious changes."]],"check":{"q":"A central logging account must receive objects from many source accounts. Which control is central to granting cross-account writes?","options":["A bucket policy on the destination bucket","A local security group","An EC2 placement group","A Route 53 health check"],"correct":0,"explain":"S3 bucket policies are resource policies that can authorize principals from other accounts."}},{"title":"Architect view: Advanced Amazon S3","duration":9,"lead":"Turn the basic service knowledge into a repeatable architecture decision.","plain":"A solutions architect starts with the requirement rather than a favorite service. Choose lifecycle for cost and retention automation, replication for a second managed copy, versioning for recoverability, and events when object changes should start work. Be careful of this common mistake: Assuming replication automatically copies historical objects or replaces a deliberate backup strategy.","analogy":"A good architect is like a doctor: understand the symptoms and constraints before choosing the treatment.","flow":["Read the requirement","Remove unsuitable choices","Compare tradeoffs","Explain the final choice"],"points":["S3 authorization model: Combine IAM, bucket policies, access points, ACL considerations, and Block Public Access.","SSE-S3, SSE-KMS, and client-side encryption: Choose encryption and understand key-control tradeoffs.","Versioning and Object Lock: Protect objects from accidental or malicious changes.","Cross-Region and same-Region replication: Replicate selected objects for compliance, latency, or account isolation.","Decision rule: Choose lifecycle for cost and retention automation, replication for a second managed copy, versioning for recoverability, and events when object changes should start work."],"example":"Enable versioning, encrypt objects, restrict access, replicate important data, and transition old objects to a cheaper class.","terms":[["Cross-Region and same-Region replication","Replicate selected objects for compliance, latency, or account isolation."],["Lifecycle rules","Transition and expire current or noncurrent objects."],["Event notifications","Trigger SQS, SNS, Lambda, or EventBridge workflows from object events."]],"check":{"q":"What should guide the architecture choice in this section?","options":["The stated requirements and tradeoffs","The newest AWS service","The option with the longest name","Using every available feature"],"correct":0,"explain":"Service choices should be justified by requirements, constraints, and tradeoffs—not novelty or feature count."},"lab":"s3-lifecycle.html"}]},{"title":"Amazon S3 Security","subtitle":"Protect S3 data with policies, encryption, access controls, and private access.","lessons":[{"title":"Start here: Amazon S3 Security","duration":8,"lead":"Control who can access S3 data, how it is encrypted, and how private workloads reach it.","plain":"In this section, focus first on the job these services perform. Control who can access S3 data, how it is encrypted, and how private workloads reach it. Do not memorize every option yet; learn the basic problem, the normal solution, and the vocabulary used in architecture diagrams.","analogy":"Advanced S3 is like running warehouse rules: who may enter, where copies go, and when old items move or expire.","flow":["S3 authorization model","SSE-S3, SSE-KMS, and client-side encryption","Versioning and Object Lock","Cross-Region and same-Region replication"],"points":["Block Public Access helps prevent accidental public exposure.","Bucket policies are resource policies and can enforce conditions such as TLS or a VPC endpoint.","S3 supports server-side encryption options integrated with AWS KMS.","Access Points can provide separate access patterns for shared data sets."],"example":"Enable versioning, encrypt objects, restrict access, replicate important data, and transition old objects to a cheaper class.","terms":[["S3 authorization model","Combine IAM, bucket policies, access points, ACL considerations, and Block Public Access."],["SSE-S3, SSE-KMS, and client-side encryption","Choose encryption and understand key-control tradeoffs."],["Versioning and Object Lock","Protect objects from accidental or malicious changes."]],"check":{"q":"A central logging account must receive objects from many source accounts. Which control is central to granting cross-account writes?","options":["A bucket policy on the destination bucket","A local security group","An EC2 placement group","A Route 53 health check"],"correct":0,"explain":"S3 bucket policies are resource policies that can authorize principals from other accounts."}},{"title":"Architect view: Amazon S3 Security","duration":9,"lead":"Turn the basic service knowledge into a repeatable architecture decision.","plain":"A solutions architect starts with the requirement rather than a favorite service. Keep buckets private by default, grant least privilege, require encryption in transit, select the needed encryption control, and use endpoints for private network access where appropriate. Be careful of this common mistake: Making a bucket public to solve an application permission problem.","analogy":"A good architect is like a doctor: understand the symptoms and constraints before choosing the treatment.","flow":["Read the requirement","Remove unsuitable choices","Compare tradeoffs","Explain the final choice"],"points":["S3 authorization model: Combine IAM, bucket policies, access points, ACL considerations, and Block Public Access.","SSE-S3, SSE-KMS, and client-side encryption: Choose encryption and understand key-control tradeoffs.","Versioning and Object Lock: Protect objects from accidental or malicious changes.","Cross-Region and same-Region replication: Replicate selected objects for compliance, latency, or account isolation.","Decision rule: Keep buckets private by default, grant least privilege, require encryption in transit, select the needed encryption control, and use endpoints for private network access where appropriate."],"example":"Enable versioning, encrypt objects, restrict access, replicate important data, and transition old objects to a cheaper class.","terms":[["Cross-Region and same-Region replication","Replicate selected objects for compliance, latency, or account isolation."],["Lifecycle rules","Transition and expire current or noncurrent objects."],["Event notifications","Trigger SQS, SNS, Lambda, or EventBridge workflows from object events."]],"check":{"q":"What should guide the architecture choice in this section?","options":["The stated requirements and tradeoffs","The newest AWS service","The option with the longest name","Using every available feature"],"correct":0,"explain":"Service choices should be justified by requirements, constraints, and tradeoffs—not novelty or feature count."},"lab":"s3-lifecycle.html"}]},{"title":"CloudFront & AWS Global Accelerator","subtitle":"Improve global delivery and route traffic over the AWS global network.","lessons":[{"title":"Start here: CloudFront & AWS Global Accelerator","duration":8,"lead":"Improve global performance and security with caching, edge routing, origin controls, and acceleration.","plain":"In this section, focus first on the job these services perform. Improve global performance and security with caching, edge routing, origin controls, and acceleration. Do not memorize every option yet; learn the basic problem, the normal solution, and the vocabulary used in architecture diagrams.","analogy":"CloudFront is like placing popular products in local depots closer to customers.","flow":["CloudFront distributions and origins","Cache behaviors, keys, and TTL","Origin security and signed access","Lambda@Edge and CloudFront Functions"],"points":["CloudFront is a content delivery network that caches content at edge locations.","Origin Access Control helps restrict an S3 origin so users access content through CloudFront.","Global Accelerator uses the AWS global network to route TCP or UDP traffic to optimal healthy regional endpoints.","Cache keys and TTLs determine how CloudFront reuses cached responses."],"example":"Place CloudFront in front of an S3 or ALB origin so cached content is served closer to users.","terms":[["CloudFront distributions and origins","Connect S3, ALB, EC2, and custom origins to edge locations."],["Cache behaviors, keys, and TTL","Control caching by path, headers, cookies, and query strings."],["Origin security and signed access","Use OAC, signed URLs, and signed cookies for controlled delivery."]],"check":{"q":"A global application requires two static anycast IP addresses and fast routing to healthy regional endpoints for TCP traffic. What fits?","options":["Amazon CloudFront only","AWS Global Accelerator","S3 Transfer Acceleration","Route 53 private hosted zone"],"correct":1,"explain":"Global Accelerator provides static anycast IPs and uses the AWS global network for TCP/UDP endpoint routing."}},{"title":"Architect view: CloudFront & AWS Global Accelerator","duration":9,"lead":"Turn the basic service knowledge into a repeatable architecture decision.","plain":"A solutions architect starts with the requirement rather than a favorite service. Use CloudFront for HTTP caching and edge features; use Global Accelerator for non-cacheable TCP/UDP or static anycast IP requirements. Be careful of this common mistake: Choosing Global Accelerator solely to cache static website assets.","analogy":"A good architect is like a doctor: understand the symptoms and constraints before choosing the treatment.","flow":["Read the requirement","Remove unsuitable choices","Compare tradeoffs","Explain the final choice"],"points":["CloudFront distributions and origins: Connect S3, ALB, EC2, and custom origins to edge locations.","Cache behaviors, keys, and TTL: Control caching by path, headers, cookies, and query strings.","Origin security and signed access: Use OAC, signed URLs, and signed cookies for controlled delivery.","Lambda@Edge and CloudFront Functions: Run lightweight request logic at edge points.","Decision rule: Use CloudFront for HTTP caching and edge features; use Global Accelerator for non-cacheable TCP/UDP or static anycast IP requirements."],"example":"Place CloudFront in front of an S3 or ALB origin so cached content is served closer to users.","terms":[["Lambda@Edge and CloudFront Functions","Run lightweight request logic at edge points."],["Global Accelerator","Accelerate global TCP/UDP applications and simplify endpoint failover."],["Tradeoff","A benefit gained while accepting a cost or limitation."]],"check":{"q":"What should guide the architecture choice in this section?","options":["The stated requirements and tradeoffs","The newest AWS service","The option with the longest name","Using every available feature"],"correct":0,"explain":"Service choices should be justified by requirements, constraints, and tradeoffs—not novelty or feature count."},"lab":"route53-delivery.html"}]},{"title":"AWS Storage Extras","subtitle":"Recognize specialized file, hybrid, transfer, and backup storage choices.","lessons":[{"title":"Start here: AWS Storage Extras","duration":8,"lead":"Recognize additional storage and transfer services used for Windows files, high-performance computing, hybrid access, backups, and data movement.","plain":"In this section, focus first on the job these services perform. Recognize additional storage and transfer services used for Windows files, high-performance computing, hybrid access, backups, and data movement. Do not memorize every option yet; learn the basic problem, the normal solution, and the vocabulary used in architecture diagrams.","analogy":"Storage choices are like a laptop disk, a temporary workbench, or a shared company drive—each solves a different problem.","flow":["EBS volume types and performance","EBS snapshots and encryption","Instance store","Amazon EFS"],"points":["FSx provides managed file systems for Windows, Lustre, ONTAP, and OpenZFS use cases.","Storage Gateway connects on-premises environments to AWS storage through file, volume, or tape patterns.","DataSync accelerates managed online data transfer between storage locations.","AWS Backup centralizes policy-based backups for supported services."],"example":"Use EBS for one server's persistent boot disk and EFS when many Linux servers need the same shared files.","terms":[["EBS volume types and performance","Compare general-purpose SSD, provisioned IOPS, throughput HDD, and cold HDD."],["EBS snapshots and encryption","Protect volumes, copy snapshots, and understand encryption inheritance."],["Instance store","Use ephemeral local disks for caches, buffers, and scratch data."]],"check":{"q":"Hundreds of Linux EC2 instances need concurrent access to the same POSIX file system. Which service is the normal choice?","options":["Amazon EBS","Amazon EFS","Instance store","Amazon S3 Glacier"],"correct":1,"explain":"EFS is a managed NFS service built for shared file access from multiple Linux clients."}},{"title":"Architect view: AWS Storage Extras","duration":9,"lead":"Turn the basic service knowledge into a repeatable architecture decision.","plain":"A solutions architect starts with the requirement rather than a favorite service. Start with the required protocol and location: choose FSx for specialized file systems, Storage Gateway for hybrid access, DataSync for managed transfer, and AWS Backup for centralized backup policy. Be careful of this common mistake: Choosing storage only by capacity while ignoring protocol, latency, sharing, and location requirements.","analogy":"A good architect is like a doctor: understand the symptoms and constraints before choosing the treatment.","flow":["Read the requirement","Remove unsuitable choices","Compare tradeoffs","Explain the final choice"],"points":["EBS volume types and performance: Compare general-purpose SSD, provisioned IOPS, throughput HDD, and cold HDD.","EBS snapshots and encryption: Protect volumes, copy snapshots, and understand encryption inheritance.","Instance store: Use ephemeral local disks for caches, buffers, and scratch data.","Amazon EFS: Design shared Linux file storage across Availability Zones.","Decision rule: Start with the required protocol and location: choose FSx for specialized file systems, Storage Gateway for hybrid access, DataSync for managed transfer, and AWS Backup for centralized backup policy."],"example":"Use EBS for one server's persistent boot disk and EFS when many Linux servers need the same shared files.","terms":[["Amazon EFS","Design shared Linux file storage across Availability Zones."],["Amazon FSx families","Select Windows, Lustre, ONTAP, or OpenZFS based on protocol and workload."],["Tradeoff","A benefit gained while accepting a cost or limitation."]],"check":{"q":"What should guide the architecture choice in this section?","options":["The stated requirements and tradeoffs","The newest AWS service","The option with the longest name","Using every available feature"],"correct":0,"explain":"Service choices should be justified by requirements, constraints, and tradeoffs—not novelty or feature count."},"lab":"storage-selector.html"}]},{"title":"Decoupling applications: SQS, SNS, Kinesis & Amazon MQ","subtitle":"Separate components so a temporary failure or traffic burst does not break the whole application.","lessons":[{"title":"Start here: Decoupling applications: SQS, SNS, Kinesis & Amazon MQ","duration":8,"lead":"Decouple systems using queues, publish/subscribe, data streams, and managed message brokers.","plain":"In this section, focus first on the job these services perform. Decouple systems using queues, publish/subscribe, data streams, and managed message brokers. Do not memorize every option yet; learn the basic problem, the normal solution, and the vocabulary used in architecture diagrams.","analogy":"Messaging services are mailboxes, broadcast systems, and event streams that let teams work independently.","flow":["SQS Standard and FIFO","Visibility timeout, long polling, and DLQs","SNS topics and subscriptions","SNS and SQS fan-out"],"points":["SQS Standard prioritizes scale and at-least-once delivery; consumers must handle duplicate processing.","SQS FIFO supports ordered processing within message groups and deduplication semantics.","SNS fans messages out to multiple subscribers and commonly pairs with SQS queues.","Kinesis Data Streams supports ordered records within shards and multiple streaming consumers."],"example":"An order API puts work in SQS so it can accept orders even while a worker is temporarily unavailable.","terms":[["SQS Standard and FIFO","Choose throughput, ordering, deduplication, and message-group behavior."],["Visibility timeout, long polling, and DLQs","Design reliable consumption, retries, and poison-message handling."],["SNS topics and subscriptions","Push events to multiple endpoints and filter subscriptions."]],"check":{"q":"Several independent services must each receive and reliably process every event at their own pace. Which pattern is best?","options":["One shared SQS queue","SNS topic fan-out to separate SQS queues","One EC2 instance polling a database","A single Lambda reserved concurrency setting"],"correct":1,"explain":"SNS-to-SQS fan-out gives each consumer its own durable queue and independent processing rate."}},{"title":"Architect view: Decoupling applications: SQS, SNS, Kinesis & Amazon MQ","duration":9,"lead":"Turn the basic service knowledge into a repeatable architecture decision.","plain":"A solutions architect starts with the requirement rather than a favorite service. Use SQS for work queues, SNS for push fan-out, Kinesis for replayable ordered streaming data, and Amazon MQ for compatible traditional broker protocols. Be careful of this common mistake: Using SNS alone when consumers must process messages later and withstand downtime without losing them.","analogy":"A good architect is like a doctor: understand the symptoms and constraints before choosing the treatment.","flow":["Read the requirement","Remove unsuitable choices","Compare tradeoffs","Explain the final choice"],"points":["SQS Standard and FIFO: Choose throughput, ordering, deduplication, and message-group behavior.","Visibility timeout, long polling, and DLQs: Design reliable consumption, retries, and poison-message handling.","SNS topics and subscriptions: Push events to multiple endpoints and filter subscriptions.","SNS and SQS fan-out: Give each consumer a durable, independently scalable event stream.","Decision rule: Use SQS for work queues, SNS for push fan-out, Kinesis for replayable ordered streaming data, and Amazon MQ for compatible traditional broker protocols."],"example":"An order API puts work in SQS so it can accept orders even while a worker is temporarily unavailable.","terms":[["SNS and SQS fan-out","Give each consumer a durable, independently scalable event stream."],["Kinesis Data Streams","Size shards and understand ordering, retention, and consumers."],["Amazon MQ","Support applications requiring standard broker protocols or migration compatibility."]],"check":{"q":"What should guide the architecture choice in this section?","options":["The stated requirements and tradeoffs","The newest AWS service","The option with the longest name","Using every available feature"],"correct":0,"explain":"Service choices should be justified by requirements, constraints, and tradeoffs—not novelty or feature count."},"lab":"sqs.html"}]},{"title":"Containers on AWS: ECS, Fargate, ECR & EKS","subtitle":"Understand containers and choose the right AWS orchestration option.","lessons":[{"title":"Start here: Containers on AWS: ECS, Fargate, ECR & EKS","duration":8,"lead":"Run containerized workloads with managed orchestration, serverless compute, and image registries.","plain":"In this section, focus first on the job these services perform. Run containerized workloads with managed orchestration, serverless compute, and image registries. Do not memorize every option yet; learn the basic problem, the normal solution, and the vocabulary used in architecture diagrams.","analogy":"A container is a standardized package; orchestration services decide where and how those packages run.","flow":["Container concepts and ECR","ECS clusters, tasks, and services","EC2 versus Fargate launch types","EKS fundamentals"],"points":["ECS is AWS-native container orchestration; EKS provides managed Kubernetes control planes.","Fargate runs ECS tasks or EKS pods without managing EC2 worker instances.","ECR stores private container images and integrates with image scanning and lifecycle policies.","ECS services maintain desired task count and integrate with load balancers and Auto Scaling."],"example":"Store a container image in ECR and run it as an ECS service on Fargate without managing EC2 hosts.","terms":[["Container concepts and ECR","Package applications into images and store them in a managed registry."],["ECS clusters, tasks, and services","Model task definitions, desired state, networking, and service deployment."],["EC2 versus Fargate launch types","Choose host control or serverless container execution."]],"check":{"q":"A team needs Kubernetes APIs but does not want to operate the Kubernetes control plane. Which service fits?","options":["Amazon ECS","Amazon EKS","AWS Batch","Amazon ECR"],"correct":1,"explain":"EKS provides a managed Kubernetes control plane while retaining Kubernetes APIs and ecosystem compatibility."}},{"title":"Architect view: Containers on AWS: ECS, Fargate, ECR & EKS","duration":9,"lead":"Turn the basic service knowledge into a repeatable architecture decision.","plain":"A solutions architect starts with the requirement rather than a favorite service. Choose ECS for simpler AWS-native orchestration, EKS when Kubernetes compatibility is required, and Fargate when removing server management matters more than maximum host-level control. Be careful of this common mistake: Assuming Fargate is a separate orchestrator rather than a compute option for ECS and EKS.","analogy":"A good architect is like a doctor: understand the symptoms and constraints before choosing the treatment.","flow":["Read the requirement","Remove unsuitable choices","Compare tradeoffs","Explain the final choice"],"points":["Container concepts and ECR: Package applications into images and store them in a managed registry.","ECS clusters, tasks, and services: Model task definitions, desired state, networking, and service deployment.","EC2 versus Fargate launch types: Choose host control or serverless container execution.","EKS fundamentals: Understand managed Kubernetes control planes and worker choices.","Decision rule: Choose ECS for simpler AWS-native orchestration, EKS when Kubernetes compatibility is required, and Fargate when removing server management matters more than maximum host-level control."],"example":"Store a container image in ECR and run it as an ECS service on Fargate without managing EC2 hosts.","terms":[["EKS fundamentals","Understand managed Kubernetes control planes and worker choices."],["App Runner and simple web services","Deploy source or container-based web apps with minimal infrastructure work."],["Tradeoff","A benefit gained while accepting a cost or limitation."]],"check":{"q":"What should guide the architecture choice in this section?","options":["The stated requirements and tradeoffs","The newest AWS service","The option with the longest name","Using every available feature"],"correct":0,"explain":"Service choices should be justified by requirements, constraints, and tradeoffs—not novelty or feature count."}}]},{"title":"Serverless Overviews from a Solution Architect Perspective","subtitle":"Run code and APIs without managing servers.","lessons":[{"title":"Start here: Serverless Overviews from a Solution Architect Perspective","duration":8,"lead":"Build event-driven serverless systems and understand scaling, integration, orchestration, and limits.","plain":"In this section, focus first on the job these services perform. Build event-driven serverless systems and understand scaling, integration, orchestration, and limits. Do not memorize every option yet; learn the basic problem, the normal solution, and the vocabulary used in architecture diagrams.","analogy":"Serverless is like calling a managed worker only when a task arrives instead of keeping a server running all day.","flow":["Lambda invocation models","Concurrency, scaling, and cold starts","Lambda networking and storage","API Gateway"],"points":["Lambda scales by creating concurrent execution environments up to available concurrency.","Reserved concurrency both guarantees and caps concurrency for a function.","API Gateway provides managed HTTP, REST, and WebSocket API front doors with authorization and throttling options.","Step Functions coordinates workflows and service integrations with state and retries."],"example":"API Gateway receives a request, Lambda runs the code, DynamoDB stores data, and Step Functions coordinates a multi-step workflow.","terms":[["Lambda invocation models","Compare synchronous, asynchronous, and event source mapping behavior."],["Concurrency, scaling, and cold starts","Estimate concurrency and control throttling with reserved or provisioned concurrency."],["Lambda networking and storage","Understand VPC access, temporary storage, layers, and file system integration."]],"check":{"q":"A multi-step order workflow needs branching, retries, waiting, and a visible execution history. What should coordinate it?","options":["An EC2 cron job","AWS Step Functions","An S3 lifecycle rule","A security group"],"correct":1,"explain":"Step Functions is designed for stateful workflow orchestration with retries, branching, waits, and execution history."}},{"title":"Architect view: Serverless Overviews from a Solution Architect Perspective","duration":9,"lead":"Turn the basic service knowledge into a repeatable architecture decision.","plain":"A solutions architect starts with the requirement rather than a favorite service. Use Lambda for event-driven short-lived compute, API Gateway for managed APIs, and Step Functions when a workflow needs explicit state, branching, retries, or coordination. Be careful of this common mistake: Using a chain of tightly coupled Lambda calls when Step Functions can model the workflow and failure behavior clearly.","analogy":"A good architect is like a doctor: understand the symptoms and constraints before choosing the treatment.","flow":["Read the requirement","Remove unsuitable choices","Compare tradeoffs","Explain the final choice"],"points":["Lambda invocation models: Compare synchronous, asynchronous, and event source mapping behavior.","Concurrency, scaling, and cold starts: Estimate concurrency and control throttling with reserved or provisioned concurrency.","Lambda networking and storage: Understand VPC access, temporary storage, layers, and file system integration.","API Gateway: Choose endpoint type, API type, authentication, caching, and throttling.","Decision rule: Use Lambda for event-driven short-lived compute, API Gateway for managed APIs, and Step Functions when a workflow needs explicit state, branching, retries, or coordination."],"example":"API Gateway receives a request, Lambda runs the code, DynamoDB stores data, and Step Functions coordinates a multi-step workflow.","terms":[["API Gateway","Choose endpoint type, API type, authentication, caching, and throttling."],["Step Functions","Model standard and express workflows with service integrations."],["Serverless architecture pattern","Combine API Gateway, Lambda, DynamoDB, queues, and observability."]],"check":{"q":"What should guide the architecture choice in this section?","options":["The stated requirements and tradeoffs","The newest AWS service","The option with the longest name","Using every available feature"],"correct":0,"explain":"Service choices should be justified by requirements, constraints, and tradeoffs—not novelty or feature count."},"lab":"lambda-concurrency.html"}]},{"title":"Serverless Solution Architecture Discussions","subtitle":"Combine Lambda, API Gateway, queues, events, and managed databases.","lessons":[{"title":"Start here: Serverless Solution Architecture Discussions","duration":8,"lead":"Combine API Gateway, Lambda, DynamoDB, SQS, SNS, EventBridge, and Step Functions into serverless application patterns.","plain":"In this section, focus first on the job these services perform. Combine API Gateway, Lambda, DynamoDB, SQS, SNS, EventBridge, and Step Functions into serverless application patterns. Do not memorize every option yet; learn the basic problem, the normal solution, and the vocabulary used in architecture diagrams.","analogy":"Architecture is a blueprint showing how users, networks, compute, storage, and data services work together.","flow":["Three-tier web architecture","Event-driven architecture","Serverless web application","Data processing pipeline"],"points":["API Gateway exposes managed HTTP APIs.","Lambda runs event-driven code and should remain stateless between invocations.","DynamoDB provides managed key-value and document storage.","Queues and workflows improve reliability when work has multiple steps or retries."],"example":"A basic web app can use Route 53, CloudFront, an ALB, Auto Scaling EC2, and a managed database in private subnets.","terms":[["Three-tier web architecture","Combine Route 53, CloudFront, ALB, Auto Scaling, and managed data services."],["Event-driven architecture","Use events, queues, topics, functions, and workflow orchestration."],["Serverless web application","Design managed front ends, APIs, compute, identity, and data."]],"check":{"q":"Traffic arrives in bursts and backend workers process jobs at a steady rate. Which component best decouples the rates?","options":["Amazon SQS","A larger DNS TTL","A network ACL","An EBS snapshot"],"correct":0,"explain":"SQS buffers work so producers and consumers can operate at different rates and recover independently."}},{"title":"Architect view: Serverless Solution Architecture Discussions","duration":9,"lead":"Turn the basic service knowledge into a repeatable architecture decision.","plain":"A solutions architect starts with the requirement rather than a favorite service. Use synchronous calls only when the user must wait for an immediate result; use queues, events, and workflows when work can be decoupled or retried. Be careful of this common mistake: Building a long synchronous chain of Lambda functions where a workflow or queue would provide clearer retries and failure handling.","analogy":"A good architect is like a doctor: understand the symptoms and constraints before choosing the treatment.","flow":["Read the requirement","Remove unsuitable choices","Compare tradeoffs","Explain the final choice"],"points":["Three-tier web architecture: Combine Route 53, CloudFront, ALB, Auto Scaling, and managed data services.","Event-driven architecture: Use events, queues, topics, functions, and workflow orchestration.","Serverless web application: Design managed front ends, APIs, compute, identity, and data.","Data processing pipeline: Ingest, buffer, transform, catalog, store, and analyze data.","Decision rule: Use synchronous calls only when the user must wait for an immediate result; use queues, events, and workflows when work can be decoupled or retried."],"example":"A basic web app can use Route 53, CloudFront, an ALB, Auto Scaling EC2, and a managed database in private subnets.","terms":[["Data processing pipeline","Ingest, buffer, transform, catalog, store, and analyze data."],["Hybrid architecture","Integrate on-premises identity, networks, DNS, storage, and migration."],["Service-selection method","Compare options against protocol, consistency, scale, resilience, operations, and cost."]],"check":{"q":"What should guide the architecture choice in this section?","options":["The stated requirements and tradeoffs","The newest AWS service","The option with the longest name","Using every available feature"],"correct":0,"explain":"Service choices should be justified by requirements, constraints, and tradeoffs—not novelty or feature count."},"lab":"autoscaling.html"}]},{"title":"Databases in AWS","subtitle":"Compare relational, key-value, document, graph, time-series, and warehouse databases.","lessons":[{"title":"Start here: Databases in AWS","duration":8,"lead":"Select purpose-built database services for document, graph, ledger, time-series, search, and warehouse workloads.","plain":"In this section, focus first on the job these services perform. Select purpose-built database services for document, graph, ledger, time-series, search, and warehouse workloads. Do not memorize every option yet; learn the basic problem, the normal solution, and the vocabulary used in architecture diagrams.","analogy":"Choosing a database is like choosing a filing system: tables, key-value lookups, graphs, and time series suit different questions.","flow":["DocumentDB and document data","Neptune graph database","Keyspaces, QLDB, and Timestream","OpenSearch Service"],"points":["DocumentDB provides MongoDB-compatible document database capabilities but is not identical to self-managed MongoDB.","Neptune is optimized for graph relationships and graph query languages.","Timestream is designed for time-series data.","Redshift is a managed data warehouse for analytical queries rather than a transactional OLTP replacement."],"example":"Use DynamoDB for predictable key-based access, Neptune for graph relationships, and Redshift for analytical warehouse queries.","terms":[["DocumentDB and document data","Identify document-oriented workloads and compatibility considerations."],["Neptune graph database","Model highly connected data and relationship traversal."],["Keyspaces, QLDB, and Timestream","Recognize Cassandra-compatible, ledger, and time-series use cases."]],"check":{"q":"A fraud system needs to traverse complex relationships among accounts and transactions. Which database is purpose-built for this?","options":["Amazon Neptune","Amazon ElastiCache","Amazon RDS for MySQL","Amazon Timestream"],"correct":0,"explain":"Neptune is a managed graph database suited to highly connected data and relationship traversal."}},{"title":"Architect view: Databases in AWS","duration":9,"lead":"Turn the basic service knowledge into a repeatable architecture decision.","plain":"A solutions architect starts with the requirement rather than a favorite service. Choose a specialized engine only when the data model and access pattern clearly match its strength; defaulting every workload to a purpose-built database adds unnecessary complexity. Be careful of this common mistake: Selecting Redshift for a latency-sensitive transactional application because it stores structured data.","analogy":"A good architect is like a doctor: understand the symptoms and constraints before choosing the treatment.","flow":["Read the requirement","Remove unsuitable choices","Compare tradeoffs","Explain the final choice"],"points":["DocumentDB and document data: Identify document-oriented workloads and compatibility considerations.","Neptune graph database: Model highly connected data and relationship traversal.","Keyspaces, QLDB, and Timestream: Recognize Cassandra-compatible, ledger, and time-series use cases.","OpenSearch Service: Support search, log analytics, and text-oriented query needs.","Decision rule: Choose a specialized engine only when the data model and access pattern clearly match its strength; defaulting every workload to a purpose-built database adds unnecessary complexity."],"example":"Use DynamoDB for predictable key-based access, Neptune for graph relationships, and Redshift for analytical warehouse queries.","terms":[["OpenSearch Service","Support search, log analytics, and text-oriented query needs."],["Redshift overview","Use a columnar data warehouse for large analytical workloads."],["Tradeoff","A benefit gained while accepting a cost or limitation."]],"check":{"q":"What should guide the architecture choice in this section?","options":["The stated requirements and tradeoffs","The newest AWS service","The option with the longest name","Using every available feature"],"correct":0,"explain":"Service choices should be justified by requirements, constraints, and tradeoffs—not novelty or feature count."},"lab":"dynamodb.html"}]},{"title":"Data & Analytics","subtitle":"Ingest, store, transform, query, and visualize large data sets.","lessons":[{"title":"Start here: Data & Analytics","duration":8,"lead":"Choose serverless query, ETL, big data, streaming, catalog, and visualization services.","plain":"In this section, focus first on the job these services perform. Choose serverless query, ETL, big data, streaming, catalog, and visualization services. Do not memorize every option yet; learn the basic problem, the normal solution, and the vocabulary used in architecture diagrams.","analogy":"Analytics is a factory line that collects raw data, cleans it, organizes it, and turns it into useful answers.","flow":["Athena and S3 analytics","Glue Data Catalog and ETL","EMR","Redshift and Redshift Spectrum"],"points":["Athena runs serverless SQL queries directly against data in S3.","Glue provides a data catalog and managed ETL capabilities.","EMR runs big-data frameworks such as Spark and Hadoop with more cluster-level control.","Lake Formation helps centrally govern and build data lakes on AWS."],"example":"Collect events with Kinesis, store them in S3, catalog with Glue, and query with Athena.","terms":[["Athena and S3 analytics","Query files directly and improve cost with partitioning and columnar formats."],["Glue Data Catalog and ETL","Discover schemas, catalog data, and run managed transformations."],["EMR","Run Spark, Hadoop, and related frameworks with customizable cluster control."]],"check":{"q":"Analysts need occasional SQL queries over Parquet files already stored in S3 with no servers to manage. What fits?","options":["Amazon Athena","Amazon RDS Multi-AZ","Amazon MQ","AWS Direct Connect"],"correct":0,"explain":"Athena is a serverless interactive query service for data stored in S3."}},{"title":"Architect view: Data & Analytics","duration":9,"lead":"Turn the basic service knowledge into a repeatable architecture decision.","plain":"A solutions architect starts with the requirement rather than a favorite service. Use Athena for ad hoc SQL on S3, Glue for catalog and ETL, EMR for customizable big-data frameworks, and Redshift for a managed warehouse. Be careful of this common mistake: Provisioning an EMR cluster for occasional simple SQL queries over S3 when Athena meets the need.","analogy":"A good architect is like a doctor: understand the symptoms and constraints before choosing the treatment.","flow":["Read the requirement","Remove unsuitable choices","Compare tradeoffs","Explain the final choice"],"points":["Athena and S3 analytics: Query files directly and improve cost with partitioning and columnar formats.","Glue Data Catalog and ETL: Discover schemas, catalog data, and run managed transformations.","EMR: Run Spark, Hadoop, and related frameworks with customizable cluster control.","Redshift and Redshift Spectrum: Build warehouses and query external S3 data.","Decision rule: Use Athena for ad hoc SQL on S3, Glue for catalog and ETL, EMR for customizable big-data frameworks, and Redshift for a managed warehouse."],"example":"Collect events with Kinesis, store them in S3, catalog with Glue, and query with Athena.","terms":[["Redshift and Redshift Spectrum","Build warehouses and query external S3 data."],["Lake Formation","Centralize data-lake permissions and governance."],["QuickSight and streaming analytics","Visualize insights and recognize streaming processing choices."]],"check":{"q":"What should guide the architecture choice in this section?","options":["The stated requirements and tradeoffs","The newest AWS service","The option with the longest name","Using every available feature"],"correct":0,"explain":"Service choices should be justified by requirements, constraints, and tradeoffs—not novelty or feature count."}}]},{"title":"Machine Learning","subtitle":"Recognize purpose-built AI services and the role of SageMaker.","lessons":[{"title":"Start here: Machine Learning","duration":8,"lead":"Recognize the AWS managed AI and machine-learning services that commonly appear in architecture scenarios.","plain":"In this section, focus first on the job these services perform. Recognize the AWS managed AI and machine-learning services that commonly appear in architecture scenarios. Do not memorize every option yet; learn the basic problem, the normal solution, and the vocabulary used in architecture diagrams.","analogy":"Managed AI services are specialist assistants; SageMaker is a workshop for building a custom assistant.","flow":["Understand the requirement","Learn the main service job","See a simple example","Check the idea"],"points":["SageMaker supports building, training, and deploying machine-learning models.","Rekognition analyzes images and video, while Textract extracts text and structure from documents.","Comprehend analyzes text, Transcribe converts speech to text, and Polly converts text to speech.","For the associate exam, focus mainly on choosing an existing managed capability rather than training algorithms from scratch."],"example":"Use Rekognition for image labels, Textract for scanned forms, and SageMaker when a custom model must be trained.","terms":[["Purpose-built AI services","Match image, text, speech, translation, search, and document tasks to managed APIs."],["Amazon SageMaker","Understand when a team needs a platform for custom model development and deployment."],["Architecture tradeoffs","Compare speed of implementation, customization, data location, and operational effort."]],"check":{"q":"Which service is designed to extract text and table data from scanned documents?","options":["Amazon Textract","Amazon Route 53","Amazon EBS","AWS Direct Connect"],"correct":0,"explain":"Textract is the managed document text and structure extraction service."}},{"title":"Architect view: Machine Learning","duration":9,"lead":"Turn the basic service knowledge into a repeatable architecture decision.","plain":"A solutions architect starts with the requirement rather than a favorite service. Choose the narrow managed AI service when the requirement clearly describes its job; choose SageMaker when the team must build or operate a custom model. Be careful of this common mistake: Selecting SageMaker for a simple requirement already covered by a purpose-built AI service.","analogy":"A good architect is like a doctor: understand the symptoms and constraints before choosing the treatment.","flow":["Read the requirement","Remove unsuitable choices","Compare tradeoffs","Explain the final choice"],"points":["Purpose-built AI services: Match image, text, speech, translation, search, and document tasks to managed APIs.","Amazon SageMaker: Understand when a team needs a platform for custom model development and deployment.","Architecture tradeoffs: Compare speed of implementation, customization, data location, and operational effort.","Decision rule: Choose the narrow managed AI service when the requirement clearly describes its job; choose SageMaker when the team must build or operate a custom model.","Common mistake: Selecting SageMaker for a simple requirement already covered by a purpose-built AI service."],"example":"Use Rekognition for image labels, Textract for scanned forms, and SageMaker when a custom model must be trained.","terms":[["Purpose-built AI services","Match image, text, speech, translation, search, and document tasks to managed APIs."],["Amazon SageMaker","Understand when a team needs a platform for custom model development and deployment."],["Architecture tradeoffs","Compare speed of implementation, customization, data location, and operational effort."]],"check":{"q":"What should guide the architecture choice in this section?","options":["The stated requirements and tradeoffs","The newest AWS service","The option with the longest name","Using every available feature"],"correct":0,"explain":"Service choices should be justified by requirements, constraints, and tradeoffs—not novelty or feature count."}}]},{"title":"AWS Monitoring & Audit: CloudWatch, CloudTrail & Config","subtitle":"Observe system health, record API activity, and track configuration changes.","lessons":[{"title":"Start here: AWS Monitoring & Audit: CloudWatch, CloudTrail & Config","duration":8,"lead":"Observe workloads, audit API activity, react to events, and evaluate resource configuration.","plain":"In this section, focus first on the job these services perform. Observe workloads, audit API activity, react to events, and evaluate resource configuration. Do not memorize every option yet; learn the basic problem, the normal solution, and the vocabulary used in architecture diagrams.","analogy":"Operations tools are dashboards, security cameras, event dispatchers, and configuration inspectors for your AWS environment.","flow":["CloudWatch metrics and alarms","CloudWatch Logs and insights","CloudTrail","EventBridge"],"points":["CloudWatch collects metrics, logs, alarms, dashboards, and related observability data.","CloudTrail records AWS API activity and supports governance and investigation.","EventBridge routes events from AWS services, SaaS applications, and custom producers to targets.","AWS Config records configuration changes and evaluates resources against rules."],"example":"CloudWatch alarms on errors, CloudTrail records who changed a resource, and Config detects a noncompliant setting.","terms":[["CloudWatch metrics and alarms","Track service metrics, custom metrics, dimensions, and alarm evaluation."],["CloudWatch Logs and insights","Centralize logs, query them, and define retention."],["CloudTrail","Audit management and data events across accounts and Regions."]],"check":{"q":"A security team needs to determine who changed an S3 bucket policy. Which service is the primary source?","options":["CloudWatch metrics","AWS CloudTrail","Amazon Route 53","AWS Auto Scaling"],"correct":1,"explain":"CloudTrail records API calls and identity context, making it the normal audit source for configuration changes."}},{"title":"Architect view: AWS Monitoring & Audit: CloudWatch, CloudTrail & Config","duration":9,"lead":"Turn the basic service knowledge into a repeatable architecture decision.","plain":"A solutions architect starts with the requirement rather than a favorite service. Use CloudWatch for operational telemetry, CloudTrail for API audit history, EventBridge for event routing, and Config for resource configuration history and compliance. Be careful of this common mistake: Using CloudTrail as the primary source for application performance metrics.","analogy":"A good architect is like a doctor: understand the symptoms and constraints before choosing the treatment.","flow":["Read the requirement","Remove unsuitable choices","Compare tradeoffs","Explain the final choice"],"points":["CloudWatch metrics and alarms: Track service metrics, custom metrics, dimensions, and alarm evaluation.","CloudWatch Logs and insights: Centralize logs, query them, and define retention.","CloudTrail: Audit management and data events across accounts and Regions.","EventBridge: Build event buses, rules, schedules, archives, and replay patterns.","Decision rule: Use CloudWatch for operational telemetry, CloudTrail for API audit history, EventBridge for event routing, and Config for resource configuration history and compliance."],"example":"CloudWatch alarms on errors, CloudTrail records who changed a resource, and Config detects a noncompliant setting.","terms":[["EventBridge","Build event buses, rules, schedules, archives, and replay patterns."],["AWS Config","Track resource configuration and evaluate compliance rules."],["X-Ray and tracing","Trace distributed requests and find latency or error bottlenecks."]],"check":{"q":"What should guide the architecture choice in this section?","options":["The stated requirements and tradeoffs","The newest AWS service","The option with the longest name","Using every available feature"],"correct":0,"explain":"Service choices should be justified by requirements, constraints, and tradeoffs—not novelty or feature count."},"lab":"cloudwatch.html"}]},{"title":"Identity and Access Management (IAM) - Advanced","subtitle":"Design federation, multiple accounts, central access, and guardrails.","lessons":[{"title":"Start here: Identity and Access Management (IAM) - Advanced","duration":8,"lead":"Design federation, centralized access, account separation, and governance across an AWS organization.","plain":"In this section, focus first on the job these services perform. Design federation, centralized access, account separation, and governance across an AWS organization. Do not memorize every option yet; learn the basic problem, the normal solution, and the vocabulary used in architecture diagrams.","analogy":"A multi-account AWS organization is like a company with departments, central identity, and company-wide rules.","flow":["Federation and Identity Center","AWS Organizations and OU design","SCPs and permission guardrails","Control Tower landing zones"],"points":["IAM Identity Center centralizes workforce access to multiple accounts and applications.","AWS Organizations supports account grouping, consolidated billing, and policy-based governance.","Control Tower provides a governed multi-account landing zone with guardrails.","Resource Access Manager shares supported resources across accounts without duplicating them."],"example":"Employees use IAM Identity Center to enter separate production and development accounts with centrally managed permission sets.","terms":[["Federation and Identity Center","Provide temporary workforce access from a central identity source."],["AWS Organizations and OU design","Group accounts and apply governance by organizational unit."],["SCPs and permission guardrails","Limit maximum permissions without directly granting access."]],"check":{"q":"A company needs centralized employee access to dozens of AWS accounts using its corporate identity provider. What is the normal solution?","options":["IAM Identity Center","One shared root user","Public S3 access","A NAT gateway"],"correct":0,"explain":"IAM Identity Center is designed to centralize workforce access across AWS accounts and integrate with identity providers."}},{"title":"Architect view: Identity and Access Management (IAM) - Advanced","duration":9,"lead":"Turn the basic service knowledge into a repeatable architecture decision.","plain":"A solutions architect starts with the requirement rather than a favorite service. Separate workloads and environments into accounts when you need strong boundaries, then centralize human access and apply organizational guardrails. Be careful of this common mistake: Putting every environment in one account and relying only on tags when the requirement calls for strong isolation.","analogy":"A good architect is like a doctor: understand the symptoms and constraints before choosing the treatment.","flow":["Read the requirement","Remove unsuitable choices","Compare tradeoffs","Explain the final choice"],"points":["Federation and Identity Center: Provide temporary workforce access from a central identity source.","AWS Organizations and OU design: Group accounts and apply governance by organizational unit.","SCPs and permission guardrails: Limit maximum permissions without directly granting access.","Control Tower landing zones: Automate governed account creation and preventive or detective controls.","Decision rule: Separate workloads and environments into accounts when you need strong boundaries, then centralize human access and apply organizational guardrails."],"example":"Employees use IAM Identity Center to enter separate production and development accounts with centrally managed permission sets.","terms":[["Control Tower landing zones","Automate governed account creation and preventive or detective controls."],["AWS RAM and cross-account resources","Share supported resources while retaining account isolation."],["Tradeoff","A benefit gained while accepting a cost or limitation."]],"check":{"q":"What should guide the architecture choice in this section?","options":["The stated requirements and tradeoffs","The newest AWS service","The option with the longest name","Using every available feature"],"correct":0,"explain":"Service choices should be justified by requirements, constraints, and tradeoffs—not novelty or feature count."}}]},{"title":"AWS Security & Encryption: KMS, SSM Parameter Store, Shield, WAF","subtitle":"Protect data, secrets, applications, and accounts with managed security services.","lessons":[{"title":"Start here: AWS Security & Encryption: KMS, SSM Parameter Store, Shield, WAF","duration":8,"lead":"Protect data and applications using KMS, CloudHSM, secrets services, certificates, threat detection, and edge defenses.","plain":"In this section, focus first on the job these services perform. Protect data and applications using KMS, CloudHSM, secrets services, certificates, threat detection, and edge defenses. Do not memorize every option yet; learn the basic problem, the normal solution, and the vocabulary used in architecture diagrams.","analogy":"Security services are layers of locks, key management, alarms, filters, and investigation tools.","flow":["KMS keys and envelope encryption","CloudHSM","Secrets Manager and Parameter Store","ACM and TLS"],"points":["KMS manages encryption keys and integrates with many AWS services.","Secrets Manager stores secrets and supports rotation workflows; Parameter Store handles configuration and can store encrypted secure strings.","ACM provisions and manages certificates for supported AWS integrations.","WAF filters web requests, Shield addresses DDoS protection, and GuardDuty detects threats from telemetry."],"example":"Store a database password in Secrets Manager, encrypt data with KMS, and place WAF in front of a web application.","terms":[["KMS keys and envelope encryption","Understand managed keys, customer-managed keys, grants, policies, and service integration."],["CloudHSM","Use dedicated hardware security modules when exclusive key control is required."],["Secrets Manager and Parameter Store","Store credentials or configuration and choose appropriate rotation and cost characteristics."]],"check":{"q":"An application database password must be stored securely and rotated automatically. Which service best fits?","options":["AWS Secrets Manager","Amazon GuardDuty","AWS Shield Standard","Amazon Route 53"],"correct":0,"explain":"Secrets Manager is designed to store secrets and can coordinate automated rotation."}},{"title":"Architect view: AWS Security & Encryption: KMS, SSM Parameter Store, Shield, WAF","duration":9,"lead":"Turn the basic service knowledge into a repeatable architecture decision.","plain":"A solutions architect starts with the requirement rather than a favorite service. Choose the narrow service matching the control: key management, secret storage, certificate management, web filtering, DDoS protection, or threat detection. Be careful of this common mistake: Using KMS to store application passwords directly instead of storing the secret in Secrets Manager or another suitable store and using KMS for encryption.","analogy":"A good architect is like a doctor: understand the symptoms and constraints before choosing the treatment.","flow":["Read the requirement","Remove unsuitable choices","Compare tradeoffs","Explain the final choice"],"points":["KMS keys and envelope encryption: Understand managed keys, customer-managed keys, grants, policies, and service integration.","CloudHSM: Use dedicated hardware security modules when exclusive key control is required.","Secrets Manager and Parameter Store: Store credentials or configuration and choose appropriate rotation and cost characteristics.","ACM and TLS: Provision certificates and encrypt data in transit with supported services.","Decision rule: Choose the narrow service matching the control: key management, secret storage, certificate management, web filtering, DDoS protection, or threat detection."],"example":"Store a database password in Secrets Manager, encrypt data with KMS, and place WAF in front of a web application.","terms":[["ACM and TLS","Provision certificates and encrypt data in transit with supported services."],["WAF, Shield, and Firewall Manager","Protect web applications and centrally manage edge security policies."],["GuardDuty, Inspector, Macie, and Security Hub","Detect threats, vulnerabilities, sensitive data, and centralize findings."]],"check":{"q":"What should guide the architecture choice in this section?","options":["The stated requirements and tradeoffs","The newest AWS service","The option with the longest name","Using every available feature"],"correct":0,"explain":"Service choices should be justified by requirements, constraints, and tradeoffs—not novelty or feature count."}}]},{"title":"Networking - VPC","subtitle":"Build private networks, subnets, routes, internet access, and security layers.","lessons":[{"title":"Start here: Networking - VPC","duration":8,"lead":"Design subnets, routes, internet access, private egress, and layered network controls.","plain":"In this section, focus first on the job these services perform. Design subnets, routes, internet access, private egress, and layered network controls. Do not memorize every option yet; learn the basic problem, the normal solution, and the vocabulary used in architecture diagrams.","analogy":"A VPC is a private campus with address ranges, roads, gates, public areas, and private buildings.","flow":["CIDR and subnet design","Route tables and route selection","Internet gateways and public subnets","NAT gateways and private egress"],"points":["A subnet is associated with one route table and exists in one Availability Zone.","An internet gateway enables internet routing for appropriately addressed resources; it does not make a private instance public by itself.","A NAT gateway allows outbound internet access from private IPv4 subnets without accepting unsolicited inbound connections.","VPC peering provides private routing between two VPCs but is not transitive."],"example":"Put a load balancer in public subnets and application servers in private subnets that use a NAT gateway only for outbound updates.","terms":[["CIDR and subnet design","Allocate non-overlapping address ranges and size subnets for growth."],["Route tables and route selection","Understand longest-prefix match and local VPC routing."],["Internet gateways and public subnets","Build direct internet connectivity for public resources."]],"check":{"q":"Private-subnet instances need outbound IPv4 internet access for updates but no unsolicited inbound internet traffic. What is normally used?","options":["A NAT gateway in a public subnet","An inbound-only internet gateway","A VPC peering connection","An S3 bucket policy"],"correct":0,"explain":"A NAT gateway provides outbound IPv4 internet connectivity for private subnets when routing is configured correctly."}},{"title":"Architect view: Networking - VPC","duration":9,"lead":"Turn the basic service knowledge into a repeatable architecture decision.","plain":"A solutions architect starts with the requirement rather than a favorite service. Design the VPC first with non-overlapping CIDRs, public and private subnets, routes, and security; then choose peering, Transit Gateway, VPN, Direct Connect, or PrivateLink only when connectivity requirements demand it. Be careful of this common mistake: Calling a subnet public only because it has a route to an internet gateway while the resource itself has no public address.","analogy":"A good architect is like a doctor: understand the symptoms and constraints before choosing the treatment.","flow":["Read the requirement","Remove unsuitable choices","Compare tradeoffs","Explain the final choice"],"points":["CIDR and subnet design: Allocate non-overlapping address ranges and size subnets for growth.","Route tables and route selection: Understand longest-prefix match and local VPC routing.","Internet gateways and public subnets: Build direct internet connectivity for public resources.","NAT gateways and private egress: Provide outbound IPv4 access from private subnets.","Decision rule: Design the VPC first with non-overlapping CIDRs, public and private subnets, routes, and security; then choose peering, Transit Gateway, VPN, Direct Connect, or PrivateLink only when connectivity requirements demand it."],"example":"Put a load balancer in public subnets and application servers in private subnets that use a NAT gateway only for outbound updates.","terms":[["NAT gateways and private egress","Provide outbound IPv4 access from private subnets."],["Security groups and network ACLs","Layer stateful instance controls with stateless subnet controls."],["VPC endpoints and PrivateLink","Access AWS or endpoint services privately without internet traversal."]],"check":{"q":"What should guide the architecture choice in this section?","options":["The stated requirements and tradeoffs","The newest AWS service","The option with the longest name","Using every available feature"],"correct":0,"explain":"Service choices should be justified by requirements, constraints, and tradeoffs—not novelty or feature count."},"lab":"vpc.html"}]},{"title":"Disaster Recovery & Migrations","subtitle":"Choose recovery and migration strategies from business requirements.","lessons":[{"title":"Start here: Disaster Recovery & Migrations","duration":8,"lead":"Select backup, recovery, data transfer, database migration, and application migration strategies.","plain":"In this section, focus first on the job these services perform. Select backup, recovery, data transfer, database migration, and application migration strategies. Do not memorize every option yet; learn the basic problem, the normal solution, and the vocabulary used in architecture diagrams.","analogy":"Disaster recovery is an emergency plan: decide what must be restored, how quickly, and how much data loss is acceptable.","flow":["RTO, RPO, backup, and restore","Pilot light and warm standby","Multi-site and regional failover","AWS Backup"],"points":["Backup and restore has the lowest ongoing cost but generally the longest recovery time.","Pilot light keeps critical core components running; warm standby maintains a scaled-down functional environment.","Multi-site active/active targets the lowest recovery time at the highest cost and complexity.","DMS migrates databases, DataSync moves file or object data, and Application Migration Service replicates servers."],"example":"A low-cost system uses backup and restore, while a minutes-level RTO may require warm standby in another Region.","terms":[["RTO, RPO, backup, and restore","Translate business recovery objectives into architecture choices."],["Pilot light and warm standby","Compare minimal core infrastructure with a scaled-down running environment."],["Multi-site and regional failover","Design active/active or active/passive multi-Region systems."]],"check":{"q":"A system can tolerate several hours of downtime and needs the lowest ongoing disaster recovery cost. Which strategy usually fits?","options":["Multi-site active/active","Warm standby","Pilot light","Backup and restore"],"correct":3,"explain":"Backup and restore minimizes ongoing infrastructure cost but has the longest recovery process."}},{"title":"Architect view: Disaster Recovery & Migrations","duration":9,"lead":"Turn the basic service knowledge into a repeatable architecture decision.","plain":"A solutions architect starts with the requirement rather than a favorite service. Start from RTO and RPO, then select backup/restore, pilot light, warm standby, or multi-site; choose migration tooling based on data type and acceptable downtime. Be careful of this common mistake: Choosing active/active multi-Region solely because it is most resilient when the requirement prioritizes low cost and tolerates hours of recovery time.","analogy":"A good architect is like a doctor: understand the symptoms and constraints before choosing the treatment.","flow":["Read the requirement","Remove unsuitable choices","Compare tradeoffs","Explain the final choice"],"points":["RTO, RPO, backup, and restore: Translate business recovery objectives into architecture choices.","Pilot light and warm standby: Compare minimal core infrastructure with a scaled-down running environment.","Multi-site and regional failover: Design active/active or active/passive multi-Region systems.","AWS Backup: Centralize policy-based backups across supported AWS services.","Decision rule: Start from RTO and RPO, then select backup/restore, pilot light, warm standby, or multi-site; choose migration tooling based on data type and acceptable downtime."],"example":"A low-cost system uses backup and restore, while a minutes-level RTO may require warm standby in another Region.","terms":[["AWS Backup","Centralize policy-based backups across supported AWS services."],["DMS and SCT","Migrate homogeneous or heterogeneous databases with schema conversion where needed."],["DataSync, Snow Family, Transfer Family, and MGN","Choose online, offline, managed transfer, or server-replication tools."]],"check":{"q":"What should guide the architecture choice in this section?","options":["The stated requirements and tradeoffs","The newest AWS service","The option with the longest name","Using every available feature"],"correct":0,"explain":"Service choices should be justified by requirements, constraints, and tradeoffs—not novelty or feature count."},"lab":"disaster-recovery.html"}]},{"title":"More Solution Architectures","subtitle":"Practice combining services for web, event-driven, serverless, data, and hybrid systems.","lessons":[{"title":"Start here: More Solution Architectures","duration":8,"lead":"Combine services into secure, resilient, high-performing, and cost-aware reference architectures.","plain":"In this section, focus first on the job these services perform. Combine services into secure, resilient, high-performing, and cost-aware reference architectures. Do not memorize every option yet; learn the basic problem, the normal solution, and the vocabulary used in architecture diagrams.","analogy":"Architecture is a blueprint showing how users, networks, compute, storage, and data services work together.","flow":["Three-tier web architecture","Event-driven architecture","Serverless web application","Data processing pipeline"],"points":["Stateless application tiers are easier to scale horizontally and replace.","Queues absorb bursts and decouple producer and consumer availability.","Caching can reduce latency and origin load but introduces freshness and invalidation tradeoffs.","Managed services usually reduce operational burden but may reduce low-level control."],"example":"A basic web app can use Route 53, CloudFront, an ALB, Auto Scaling EC2, and a managed database in private subnets.","terms":[["Three-tier web architecture","Combine Route 53, CloudFront, ALB, Auto Scaling, and managed data services."],["Event-driven architecture","Use events, queues, topics, functions, and workflow orchestration."],["Serverless web application","Design managed front ends, APIs, compute, identity, and data."]],"check":{"q":"Traffic arrives in bursts and backend workers process jobs at a steady rate. Which component best decouples the rates?","options":["Amazon SQS","A larger DNS TTL","A network ACL","An EBS snapshot"],"correct":0,"explain":"SQS buffers work so producers and consumers can operate at different rates and recover independently."}},{"title":"Architect view: More Solution Architectures","duration":9,"lead":"Turn the basic service knowledge into a repeatable architecture decision.","plain":"A solutions architect starts with the requirement rather than a favorite service. Choose the simplest architecture that meets all nonfunctional requirements, then identify the failure mode of each component and remove unnecessary single points of failure. Be careful of this common mistake: Adding components because they are common in reference diagrams even when the scenario does not require them.","analogy":"A good architect is like a doctor: understand the symptoms and constraints before choosing the treatment.","flow":["Read the requirement","Remove unsuitable choices","Compare tradeoffs","Explain the final choice"],"points":["Three-tier web architecture: Combine Route 53, CloudFront, ALB, Auto Scaling, and managed data services.","Event-driven architecture: Use events, queues, topics, functions, and workflow orchestration.","Serverless web application: Design managed front ends, APIs, compute, identity, and data.","Data processing pipeline: Ingest, buffer, transform, catalog, store, and analyze data.","Decision rule: Choose the simplest architecture that meets all nonfunctional requirements, then identify the failure mode of each component and remove unnecessary single points of failure."],"example":"A basic web app can use Route 53, CloudFront, an ALB, Auto Scaling EC2, and a managed database in private subnets.","terms":[["Data processing pipeline","Ingest, buffer, transform, catalog, store, and analyze data."],["Hybrid architecture","Integrate on-premises identity, networks, DNS, storage, and migration."],["Service-selection method","Compare options against protocol, consistency, scale, resilience, operations, and cost."]],"check":{"q":"What should guide the architecture choice in this section?","options":["The stated requirements and tradeoffs","The newest AWS service","The option with the longest name","Using every available feature"],"correct":0,"explain":"Service choices should be justified by requirements, constraints, and tradeoffs—not novelty or feature count."},"lab":"autoscaling.html"}]},{"title":"Other Services","subtitle":"Recognize supporting services without overloading the beginner path.","lessons":[{"title":"Start here: Other Services","duration":8,"lead":"Build a small recognition-level map of useful services that do not need a full deep-dive section.","plain":"In this section, focus first on the job these services perform. Build a small recognition-level map of useful services that do not need a full deep-dive section. Do not memorize every option yet; learn the basic problem, the normal solution, and the vocabulary used in architecture diagrams.","analogy":"These services are a toolbox: learn the job of each tool before learning every setting.","flow":["Understand the requirement","Learn the main service job","See a simple example","Check the idea"],"points":["CloudFormation describes infrastructure as code.","Systems Manager helps operate fleets and manage configuration without relying on direct SSH.","AWS Batch runs batch computing jobs, and AppFlow transfers data between supported SaaS applications and AWS.","SES sends email, while Step Functions coordinates multi-step workflows."],"example":"Use CloudFormation to reproduce an environment, Systems Manager to manage instances, and AWS Batch to run queued batch jobs.","terms":[["Infrastructure and operations","Recognize CloudFormation and Systems Manager."],["Batch and integration","Recognize AWS Batch, AppFlow, and managed transfer services."],["Communication and workflows","Recognize SES, Pinpoint, and Step Functions."]],"check":{"q":"Which service describes and deploys AWS infrastructure from templates?","options":["AWS CloudFormation","Amazon Polly","Amazon Route 53","AWS Shield"],"correct":0,"explain":"CloudFormation is AWS infrastructure as code."}},{"title":"Architect view: Other Services","duration":9,"lead":"Turn the basic service knowledge into a repeatable architecture decision.","plain":"A solutions architect starts with the requirement rather than a favorite service. Identify the job first—provisioning, operations, batch work, integration, communication, or orchestration—then choose the managed service for that job. Be careful of this common mistake: Trying to learn every minor feature before understanding the primary purpose of the service.","analogy":"A good architect is like a doctor: understand the symptoms and constraints before choosing the treatment.","flow":["Read the requirement","Remove unsuitable choices","Compare tradeoffs","Explain the final choice"],"points":["Infrastructure and operations: Recognize CloudFormation and Systems Manager.","Batch and integration: Recognize AWS Batch, AppFlow, and managed transfer services.","Communication and workflows: Recognize SES, Pinpoint, and Step Functions.","Decision rule: Identify the job first—provisioning, operations, batch work, integration, communication, or orchestration—then choose the managed service for that job.","Common mistake: Trying to learn every minor feature before understanding the primary purpose of the service."],"example":"Use CloudFormation to reproduce an environment, Systems Manager to manage instances, and AWS Batch to run queued batch jobs.","terms":[["Infrastructure and operations","Recognize CloudFormation and Systems Manager."],["Batch and integration","Recognize AWS Batch, AppFlow, and managed transfer services."],["Communication and workflows","Recognize SES, Pinpoint, and Step Functions."]],"check":{"q":"What should guide the architecture choice in this section?","options":["The stated requirements and tradeoffs","The newest AWS service","The option with the longest name","Using every available feature"],"correct":0,"explain":"Service choices should be justified by requirements, constraints, and tradeoffs—not novelty or feature count."}}]},{"title":"WhitePapers and Architectures","subtitle":"Use Well-Architected principles and cost awareness to review a design.","lessons":[{"title":"Start here: WhitePapers and Architectures","duration":8,"lead":"Control compute, storage, database, transfer, and operational costs while preserving requirements.","plain":"In this section, focus first on the job these services perform. Control compute, storage, database, transfer, and operational costs while preserving requirements. Do not memorize every option yet; learn the basic problem, the normal solution, and the vocabulary used in architecture diagrams.","analogy":"Well-Architected reviews are like safety and efficiency inspections for a building design.","flow":["Compute pricing and commitment","Rightsizing and elasticity","Storage and database cost","Data transfer cost"],"points":["Savings Plans and Reserved Instances trade commitment for discounts; Spot trades interruption tolerance for deep discounts.","Rightsizing and Auto Scaling reduce idle capacity.","Storage lifecycle policies align object cost with access frequency and retention.","The Well-Architected Framework evaluates operational excellence, security, reliability, performance efficiency, cost optimization, and sustainability."],"example":"Use Auto Scaling, rightsizing, storage lifecycle policies, and an appropriate commitment model without weakening required reliability.","terms":[["Compute pricing and commitment","Choose On-Demand, Spot, Savings Plans, Reserved Instances, and dedicated options."],["Rightsizing and elasticity","Measure utilization and match capacity to actual demand."],["Storage and database cost","Use storage classes, lifecycle, database sizing, and serverless capacity appropriately."]],"check":{"q":"A steady EC2 workload will run continuously for one year, but instance families may change. Which discount model is generally flexible?","options":["Compute Savings Plan","Dedicated Host only","Spot Instances","On-Demand Capacity Reservation only"],"correct":0,"explain":"Compute Savings Plans provide commitment-based discounts with broader compute flexibility than instance-specific commitments."}},{"title":"Architect view: WhitePapers and Architectures","duration":9,"lead":"Turn the basic service knowledge into a repeatable architecture decision.","plain":"A solutions architect starts with the requirement rather than a favorite service. Optimize only after preserving required reliability, security, and performance; prefer elasticity, managed services, appropriate commitments, and data lifecycle management. Be careful of this common mistake: Selecting the cheapest unit price while ignoring minimum duration, data retrieval, transfer, or operational costs.","analogy":"A good architect is like a doctor: understand the symptoms and constraints before choosing the treatment.","flow":["Read the requirement","Remove unsuitable choices","Compare tradeoffs","Explain the final choice"],"points":["Compute pricing and commitment: Choose On-Demand, Spot, Savings Plans, Reserved Instances, and dedicated options.","Rightsizing and elasticity: Measure utilization and match capacity to actual demand.","Storage and database cost: Use storage classes, lifecycle, database sizing, and serverless capacity appropriately.","Data transfer cost: Recognize cross-AZ, cross-Region, internet, NAT, and CDN cost implications.","Decision rule: Optimize only after preserving required reliability, security, and performance; prefer elasticity, managed services, appropriate commitments, and data lifecycle management."],"example":"Use Auto Scaling, rightsizing, storage lifecycle policies, and an appropriate commitment model without weakening required reliability.","terms":[["Data transfer cost","Recognize cross-AZ, cross-Region, internet, NAT, and CDN cost implications."],["Cost tools and governance","Use Cost Explorer, Budgets, CUR, tags, and anomaly detection."],["Well-Architected Framework","Review designs across the six pillars and remediate high-risk issues."]],"check":{"q":"What should guide the architecture choice in this section?","options":["The stated requirements and tradeoffs","The newest AWS service","The option with the longest name","Using every available feature"],"correct":0,"explain":"Service choices should be justified by requirements, constraints, and tradeoffs—not novelty or feature count."},"lab":"cost-optimizer.html"}]},{"title":"Preparing for the Exam + Practice Exam","subtitle":"Convert knowledge into a practical review and exam routine.","lessons":[{"title":"Start here: Preparing for the Exam + Practice Exam","duration":8,"lead":"Consolidate service comparisons, rehearse scenario reasoning, and use mock results to close knowledge gaps.","plain":"In this section, focus first on the job these services perform. Consolidate service comparisons, rehearse scenario reasoning, and use mock results to close knowledge gaps. Do not memorize every option yet; learn the basic problem, the normal solution, and the vocabulary used in architecture diagrams.","analogy":"Exam preparation is a driving test: knowing the rules matters, but you must also apply them under time pressure.","flow":["Core comparison review","Security and resilience review","Question-reading workflow","Targeted weak-area review"],"points":["Review why incorrect options fail, not only why the correct answer works.","Weak domains should guide targeted review, but the exam uses a compensatory overall score.","Time management improves when you flag long questions and return after completing easier items.","A passing practice percentage is not a direct conversion to the AWS scaled score."],"example":"Review every missed question by identifying the requirement and explaining why each distractor fails.","terms":[["Core comparison review","Revisit the high-frequency choices among compute, storage, database, networking, and integration services."],["Security and resilience review","Check identity, encryption, network controls, failover, and disaster recovery decisions."],["Question-reading workflow","Extract constraints, eliminate distractors, flag uncertainty, and manage time."]],"check":{"q":"What is the most useful way to review a missed scenario question?","options":["Memorize the answer letter","Understand the requirement and why each distractor fails","Skip all difficult topics","Retake the same question immediately until correct"],"correct":1,"explain":"Exam readiness comes from understanding requirements and architectural tradeoffs, not memorizing answer positions."}},{"title":"Architect view: Preparing for the Exam + Practice Exam","duration":9,"lead":"Turn the basic service knowledge into a repeatable architecture decision.","plain":"A solutions architect starts with the requirement rather than a favorite service. Book the exam when you can consistently explain service choices and score reliably across multiple unseen mocks—not after memorizing one question bank. Be careful of this common mistake: Repeatedly taking the same mock until answers are memorized and treating that score as readiness.","analogy":"A good architect is like a doctor: understand the symptoms and constraints before choosing the treatment.","flow":["Read the requirement","Remove unsuitable choices","Compare tradeoffs","Explain the final choice"],"points":["Core comparison review: Revisit the high-frequency choices among compute, storage, database, networking, and integration services.","Security and resilience review: Check identity, encryption, network controls, failover, and disaster recovery decisions.","Question-reading workflow: Extract constraints, eliminate distractors, flag uncertainty, and manage time.","Targeted weak-area review: Use lesson, lab, quiz, and mock data to decide what to revisit.","Decision rule: Book the exam when you can consistently explain service choices and score reliably across multiple unseen mocks—not after memorizing one question bank."],"example":"Review every missed question by identifying the requirement and explaining why each distractor fails.","terms":[["Targeted weak-area review","Use lesson, lab, quiz, and mock data to decide what to revisit."],["Full mock exam routine","Simulate exam timing and perform a complete explanation-based review."],["Tradeoff","A benefit gained while accepting a cost or limitation."]],"check":{"q":"What should guide the architecture choice in this section?","options":["The stated requirements and tradeoffs","The newest AWS service","The option with the longest name","Using every available feature"],"correct":0,"explain":"Service choices should be justified by requirements, constraints, and tradeoffs—not novelty or feature count."}}]},{"title":"Congratulations","subtitle":"Review progress and choose the next learning step.","lessons":[{"title":"Start here: Congratulations","duration":8,"lead":"Turn course completion into a review plan rather than treating the final page as the end of learning.","plain":"In this section, focus first on the job these services perform. Turn course completion into a review plan rather than treating the final page as the end of learning. Do not memorize every option yet; learn the basic problem, the normal solution, and the vocabulary used in architecture diagrams.","analogy":"Finishing the course is reaching base camp, not the summit; review and practice turn exposure into skill.","flow":["Understand the requirement","Learn the main service job","See a simple example","Check the idea"],"points":["Completion means you have seen the material, not necessarily mastered it.","Revisit sections where you cannot explain the service choice without looking at the answer.","Use new mock questions rather than memorizing one fixed set.","Keep official AWS documentation as the final source for changing product details."],"example":"If database questions remain weak, revisit RDS, DynamoDB, and database selection before taking another unseen mock.","terms":[["Review your weak areas","Use incorrect answers and uncertain lessons to guide review."],["Maintain the knowledge","Revisit core comparisons and practice architecture decisions."],["Plan the next step","Choose more labs, an official practice set, or the real exam when ready."]],"check":{"q":"What is the strongest sign that a topic is understood?","options":["You can explain why the correct service fits and why alternatives do not","You recognize the slide color","You memorized one answer letter","You opened every page once"],"correct":0,"explain":"Explanation and comparison show transferable understanding."}},{"title":"Architect view: Congratulations","duration":9,"lead":"Turn the basic service knowledge into a repeatable architecture decision.","plain":"A solutions architect starts with the requirement rather than a favorite service. Use lesson progress, lab confidence, and mock-exam domain results together to decide what to review next. Be careful of this common mistake: Booking the exam only because every lesson has a completion checkmark.","analogy":"A good architect is like a doctor: understand the symptoms and constraints before choosing the treatment.","flow":["Read the requirement","Remove unsuitable choices","Compare tradeoffs","Explain the final choice"],"points":["Review your weak areas: Use incorrect answers and uncertain lessons to guide review.","Maintain the knowledge: Revisit core comparisons and practice architecture decisions.","Plan the next step: Choose more labs, an official practice set, or the real exam when ready.","Decision rule: Use lesson progress, lab confidence, and mock-exam domain results together to decide what to review next.","Common mistake: Booking the exam only because every lesson has a completion checkmark."],"example":"If database questions remain weak, revisit RDS, DynamoDB, and database selection before taking another unseen mock.","terms":[["Review your weak areas","Use incorrect answers and uncertain lessons to guide review."],["Maintain the knowledge","Revisit core comparisons and practice architecture decisions."],["Plan the next step","Choose more labs, an official practice set, or the real exam when ready."]],"check":{"q":"What should guide the architecture choice in this section?","options":["The stated requirements and tradeoffs","The newest AWS service","The option with the longest name","Using every available feature"],"correct":0,"explain":"Service choices should be justified by requirements, constraints, and tradeoffs—not novelty or feature count."}}]}];
